/// SharingVisibilityQuestionBank — 120 questions (100 single-select, 20 multi-select)
import Foundation

enum SharingVisibilityQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A regional sales rep at Northwind Traders must update existing Opportunity records left by a departed colleague but should never be able to delete any Opportunity, even ones they own. Which permission configuration should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Assign a permission set granting Read and Edit on Opportunity, with Delete explicitly not granted, and rely on the object permission dependency rules that require Edit before Delete can be enabled."), ("B", "Assign a profile with Modify All on Opportunity so the rep can edit any record regardless of ownership, then remove the Delete checkbox from the page layout only."), ("C", "Grant View All Data so the rep can see and edit every Opportunity in the org, then use a validation rule to block deletion attempts at the record level."), ("D", "Leave the rep's existing profile alone and add a sharing rule that grants Read/Write access on Opportunities owned by the departed colleague's role.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Object permissions are independent checkboxes but Delete depends on Edit being enabled, so granting Read/Edit without Delete gives exactly the update capability required while permanently blocking deletion regardless of ownership. B is incorrect: Modify All grants delete rights implicitly and bypasses all record-level sharing. C is incorrect: View All Data doesn't grant edit rights; a validation rule isn't how delete restriction is architected. D is incorrect: A sharing rule can't remove the Delete permission already present on the rep's profile."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "An architect at a financial services org must let a permission set group give call-center agents Edit access to Contact via one member permission set, while a second member permission set in the same group grants broader access agents shouldn't have on Contact. Which mechanism should be used to remove that unwanted access without altering the underlying permission sets?", options: [("A", "Add a criteria-based sharing rule on Contact that restricts the fields agents can edit."), ("B", "Create a muting permission set inside the permission set group that suppresses the unwanted object permission from the second member permission set."), ("C", "Remove the second permission set from the group and manually recreate every other permission it grants in a brand-new permission set."), ("D", "Set the profile's object permissions on Contact to Read-Only so the aggregated result from the group is overridden.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Permission set groups support muting permission sets, which selectively suppress specific permissions granted by member permission sets without editing or removing the original permission sets. A is incorrect: Sharing rules control record-level visibility, not field- or object-level edit permissions. C is incorrect: Recreating permissions duplicates maintenance effort and breaks reusability. D is incorrect: Profiles never restrict what permission sets or groups grant; effective access is always the most permissive union."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "A nonprofit's finance director needs to see every Donation__c record company-wide for reporting, but frontline fundraisers should only see and edit their own donor's records per standard OWD-based sharing. Which TWO statements about the 'View All' object permission make it the appropriate choice for the finance director's permission set?", options: [("A", "It lets the director see every record on the object regardless of OWD or sharing rules."), ("B", "It leaves edit rights scoped to whatever object permissions and sharing otherwise apply, rather than granting edit access itself."), ("C", "It requires a public group added to a criteria-based sharing rule matching all possible field values to function."), ("D", "It is the standard way to grant read access across an object while also automatically granting edit rights.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "'View All' grants read access to every record overriding OWD (A) while leaving edit or delete rights scoped separately (B) — it doesn't itself grant edit rights unless separately assigned. C is incorrect: 'View All' requires no such sharing rule or public group configuration to function. D is incorrect: 'View All' does not grant edit rights; that would require a separate permission like 'Modify All'."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "An integration user account will run nightly batch jobs that must read every Case in the org, across all queues and owners, but the org's security policy forbids granting any integration user the ability to modify or delete records. Which permission set configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "Add the integration user to a public group referenced by a sharing rule granting Read/Write on all Cases."), ("B", "Grant Modify All on Case and rely on a validation rule to reject all DML attempts from the integration user's profile."), ("C", "Grant View All on Case only, with Create, Edit, and Delete left unchecked, satisfying full read visibility while structurally preventing any write operation."), ("D", "Grant View All Data at the system level and Edit on Case, then remove the Save button from every Case page layout.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "View All satisfies the \"read every record\" requirement while leaving Edit/Create/Delete unchecked structurally prevents any write operation. A is incorrect: A Read/Write sharing rule explicitly grants write permission, violating the stated policy. B is incorrect: Modify All grants edit/delete outright; a validation rule can be bypassed by different DML contexts. D is incorrect: Removing a layout button has no effect on API-based inserts/updates."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "A support manager at a SaaS company can see the 'Renewal Risk Score' field on the Case page layout, but a scheduled Apex integration that queries Case via SOQL never returns a value for that field even for records where the manager sees data populated. What is the most likely cause?", options: [("A", "The Renewal Risk Score field has a validation rule that blocks it from being returned in any automated query context."), ("B", "The field is a formula field and formula fields can never be queried through SOQL by any user, integration or otherwise."), ("C", "The page layout assigned to the manager includes the field while the Apex class was compiled before the field was created, permanently caching a null value."), ("D", "The field-level security for the integration user's profile has Renewal Risk Score set to invisible, so SOQL returns null for that field regardless of what the page layout shows to the manager.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Field-level security is enforced independently of page layouts and applies per running-user context; if the integration user's FLS hides the field, SOQL executed under that user's permissions returns null even though the field is populated and visible to other users. A is incorrect: Validation rules govern DML write operations, not read/query behavior. B is incorrect: Formula fields are fully queryable via SOQL; this invents a nonexistent limitation. C is incorrect: Apex classes don't cache field values at compile time based on field creation order."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "A retailer wants a subset of customer service reps to view a 'Fraud Flag' field on Account without changing the profile shared by hundreds of other reps who should never see it. Which TWO statements correctly explain why a permission set is the right tool here?", options: [("A", "A permission set granting Read on Fraud Flag can be assigned only to the subset of reps who need visibility."), ("B", "Editing the shared profile's field-level security to make Fraud Flag visible, then relying on page layout rules, achieves the same isolation."), ("C", "This approach leaves the shared profile's FLS untouched, so the hundreds of other reps remain unaffected."), ("D", "Creating a new record type for Account and exposing Fraud Flag only on that record type's layout achieves the same isolation.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "A permission set layered on top of the existing profile (A) is the standard mechanism for granting incremental field access to a subset of users, and it works precisely because it leaves the shared profile's FLS untouched (B), so the broader rep population is unaffected. B is incorrect: FLS is enforced regardless of layout, so profile-level visibility exposes the field to everyone sharing that profile. D is incorrect: Record types control layout/picklists, not field-level security."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "An automation architect needs a sensitive custom field on Case, 'Settlement Amount', to remain hidden from all human users except support managers, while still being fully readable and writable by an Apex trigger and a Flow used to compute related totals. Which design achieves this?", options: [("A", "Set Settlement Amount to Field Hidden via FLS for all profiles except support managers, and let the Apex trigger and Flow run in system context so automation is unaffected by FLS restrictions."), ("B", "Apply Shield Platform Encryption to the field so only support managers can decrypt it, and disable automation access entirely for security."), ("C", "Create two separate custom fields, one automation-only and one user-facing, and sync them nightly via a scheduled batch job."), ("D", "Remove the field from every page layout only, since removing a field from a layout also blocks it from Apex and Flow access automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "FLS restrictions apply to user-driven UI, reports, and standard SOQL access, but Apex triggers and Flows execute in system mode by default and are unaffected by profile-level FLS restrictions. B is incorrect: Shield Encryption doesn't inherently block automation access; disabling automation contradicts the requirement. C is incorrect: Duplicating fields introduces unnecessary complexity and staleness. D is incorrect: Removing a field from a layout only affects the UI, not Apex/Flow access."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "Sales reps at a manufacturing company should not see cost-basis fields on Product2 in list views, detail pages, or reports, but the finance team's nightly ETL job must still extract those fields via the Bulk API for margin analysis. Which configuration satisfies both requirements?", options: [("A", "Remove the cost-basis fields from all Product2 page layouts assigned to sales profiles, leaving FLS at the org-wide default of visible for everyone."), ("B", "Set the cost-basis fields to Field Hidden in FLS for the sales profile while granting Read via a permission set assigned to the integration user running the ETL job."), ("C", "Create a sharing rule on Product2 that hides specific field values from sales reps' role while keeping full object-level access."), ("D", "Encrypt the cost-basis fields with Classic Encryption so only users with the 'View Encrypted Data' permission, including all sales reps, can see them in reports.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "FLS is the correct enforcement point for hiding fields from all access surfaces for sales profiles, while the ETL integration user can be granted Read via its own permission set. A is incorrect: Layout-only removal doesn't stop fields appearing in reports/APIs. C is incorrect: Sharing rules operate at the record level, not the field level. D is incorrect: Classic Encryption doesn't restrict field-level read access by itself."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A telecom company's Case record type 'Escalated Billing Dispute' should present a different set of picklist values and a specialized layout to billing specialists, while regular agents using the default record type see standard values. However, both groups can still query all values through reports. What should the architect tell the compliance team about this design?", options: [("A", "Record types enforce OWD-equivalent sharing restrictions, so billing dispute Cases become invisible to non-billing profiles by default."), ("B", "Record types are a complete security boundary and no additional configuration is needed since regular agents physically cannot see billing dispute data once record types are assigned."), ("C", "Record types control which picklist values and layout appear per profile assignment, but they don't restrict data access — the same records and field values remain visible through reports, list views, and APIs regardless of record type."), ("D", "Assigning record types automatically applies field-level security matching the record type's intended audience without further configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Record types are a UI/data-integrity mechanism governing picklist values and layout assignment; they have no bearing on record- or field-level security. A is incorrect: Record types have no relationship to OWD or sharing. B is incorrect: Record types are not a security boundary; reports/APIs still expose data regardless of record type. D is incorrect: Record types don't auto-configure FLS."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "A healthcare provider must ensure a 'Diagnosis Notes' field on a custom object is encrypted at rest to satisfy HIPAA, while still supporting case-insensitive exact-match searches on the field within list views used by care coordinators. Which encryption approach should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Store the field unencrypted but restrict access solely through FLS, since FLS alone satisfies HIPAA's encryption-at-rest requirement."), ("B", "Shield Platform Encryption with probabilistic encryption, which is required whenever any searching or filtering capability on the field is needed."), ("C", "Classic Encryption, since it supports full-text search across encrypted fields out of the box for any field type."), ("D", "Shield Platform Encryption with deterministic encryption enabled for the field, which preserves the ability to perform certain filtered searches while encrypting data at rest and in transit.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Shield Platform Encryption supports deterministic encryption schemes, preserving limited filtering/exact-match capability while still encrypting data at rest, unlike probabilistic encryption. A is incorrect: FLS controls visibility, not encryption at rest. B is incorrect: Probabilistic encryption specifically prevents equality-based filtering. C is incorrect: Classic Encryption has very limited functionality and doesn't meet compliance-grade needs."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "A payment processor must prevent unauthorized viewing of cardholder data fields stored on a custom object to remain PCI compliant, while allowing a specific fraud-review permission set group to see the data and allowing standard reporting for aggregate transaction counts without exposing the raw card numbers. Which TWO layered controls, together, satisfy this?", options: [("A", "Apply Shield Platform Encryption to the cardholder data fields and restrict FLS so only the fraud-review permission set group can view decrypted values."), ("B", "Rely solely on OWD set to Private for the object, since restricting record-level access alone satisfies PCI's field-level data protection requirements."), ("C", "Use a formula field that displays only the last four digits of the card number for everyone, removing the need for encryption or FLS entirely."), ("D", "Build reports that aggregate transaction counts using non-sensitive fields instead of the encrypted ones.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "PCI compliance requires layering encryption at rest with field-level access restriction (A), combined with careful report design that avoids raw sensitive fields (B). B is incorrect: OWD governs record-level visibility, not field-level protection. C is incorrect: A masked formula field doesn't eliminate the need to protect the underlying raw field."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "A compliance team wants to detect if any user attempted to export a report containing restricted PII fields, even if the export ultimately failed due to permissions. Which native capability should the architect point to?", options: [("A", "Real-Time Event Monitoring, which can capture Report Export events including attempts, letting compliance review who attempted exports and correlate with FLS/permission failures."), ("B", "The Setup Audit Trail, which logs every individual record read and field-level view across the org in real time."), ("C", "Login History, which shows exactly which fields a user accessed once authenticated."), ("D", "Field History Tracking on the PII fields, which records every user who ever viewed the field in the UI.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Event Monitoring captures granular events like Report Export, giving compliance visibility into access attempts independent of success. B is incorrect: Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not record/field reads. C is incorrect: Login History has no visibility into which fields/reports were accessed post-login. D is incorrect: Field History Tracking logs value changes, not views."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "A multi-cloud integration synchronizes customer PII from Salesforce to an external marketing platform that has no field-level encryption support of its own. The architect must prevent raw PII from ever leaving Salesforce in a usable form. Which approach is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Enable Shield Platform Encryption on the PII fields, since encrypted values remain encrypted at every downstream system automatically, including systems that never decrypt them."), ("B", "Apply data masking or tokenization in the integration middleware layer before the PII leaves Salesforce, since native field encryption alone doesn't protect data once it's decrypted and transmitted to a system lacking equivalent controls."), ("C", "Set the external system's OWD equivalent to Private, ensuring PII is inaccessible once synced regardless of its format."), ("D", "Rely on FLS scoped to the integration user, since FLS restricts what data a middleware integration can transmit externally.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Once a user or integration with decrypt rights reads a field, plaintext is available to send onward; masking/tokenizing in middleware ensures the external system never receives usable raw PII. A is incorrect: Encrypted values are decrypted for any authorized reader before hand-off. C is incorrect: OWD is a Salesforce-native concept with no bearing on external systems. D is incorrect: FLS doesn't inspect payloads once an integration is authorized to read and transmit data."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "An Apex class processes a list of Contact records and must never expose records the running user cannot see, even though the class is invoked from a Visualforce page shared broadly across departments. Which class declaration is correct?", options: [("A", "Omit any sharing declaration, since Apex classes default to enforcing sharing rules unless a with sharing keyword is explicitly added."), ("B", "Declare the class 'without sharing', since Visualforce controllers must run in system mode to render correctly regardless of the object involved."), ("C", "Declare the class 'with sharing', which enforces the running user's record-level sharing rules for all SOQL and DML performed within that class's execution context."), ("D", "Declare the class 'inherited sharing' only when the class is never called from a trigger, since inherited sharing is unsupported outside trigger contexts.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "'With sharing' forces the class to enforce the running user's record-level sharing for its own SOQL and DML. A is incorrect: Apex classes default to 'without sharing' when no keyword is specified. B is incorrect: 'Without sharing' explicitly bypasses sharing enforcement — the opposite of the requirement. D is incorrect: 'Inherited sharing' is supported broadly, not just in triggers."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "A trigger on a custom object 'Vendor_Contract__c' must respect row-level visibility so that a user without access to a given contract cannot indirectly cause the trigger to leak data about that record via a related update. Which combination of design choices addresses this?", options: [("A", "Rely on the org-wide default of Private for Vendor_Contract__c alone, since OWD automatically propagates into Apex trigger execution context without any class-level configuration."), ("B", "Add a formula field that returns blank for unauthorized users, since formula fields respect FLS and OWD without needing any Apex-level enforcement."), ("C", "Declare the handler class 'without sharing' so the trigger can always process every related record consistently, then filter out unauthorized rows manually in a comment for future maintainers."), ("D", "Declare the trigger's handler class 'with sharing' and use WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED (or stripInaccessible()) on SOQL queries within it so both record- and field-level access are respected for the running user.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Combining a 'with sharing' handler class with WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED or stripInaccessible() ensures both record-level sharing and field-level security are actively enforced during the trigger's queries. A is incorrect: OWD doesn't automatically apply inside Apex; class-level sharing keywords determine enforcement. B is incorrect: Formula fields don't retroactively enforce OWD/sharing for the trigger's own DML. C is incorrect: 'Without sharing' bypasses record-level sharing; a comment has no runtime effect."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "A developer built a Lightning Web Component that queries Opportunity fields via an Apex method annotated @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) using a without-sharing controller class, and discovered that some sales reps can see Opportunity amounts for accounts they don't own. What change best resolves this while preserving the LWC's functionality?", options: [("A", "Change the controller class to 'with sharing' (or use WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED / stripInaccessible in the query) so the LWC's data respects the running user's actual record and field access."), ("B", "Switch the component from Lightning Web Component to Visualforce, since Visualforce automatically enforces sharing for all controller classes regardless of their declared sharing mode."), ("C", "Remove the cacheable=true annotation, since caching is the root cause of sharing bypass in Lightning Web Components."), ("D", "Add a client-side JavaScript filter in the LWC that hides Opportunity records where the user isn't the owner, without changing the Apex class.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "LWC does not automatically enforce sharing when the backing Apex class is without sharing; correcting the class's sharing declaration is the sound fix. B is incorrect: Sharing mode is controlled by class declaration, not UI technology. C is incorrect: Caching affects staleness, not sharing enforcement. D is incorrect: Client-side filtering happens after unauthorized data is already sent to the browser."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A nightly batch Apex job must have unrestricted read access across all Case records regardless of ownership to compute company-wide SLA metrics, but the same job also writes computed values back to a 'Days Open Bucket' field that has FLS restricted for the batch's running user's profile on certain sensitive Case record types. Which TWO techniques, used together, correctly solve both halves of this requirement?", options: [("A", "Declare the batch class 'with sharing' for both phases, since 'with sharing' also automatically enforces and strips inaccessible fields during DML without extra code."), ("B", "Declare the batch class 'without sharing' to bypass record-level sharing for the read phase."), ("C", "Use Security.stripInaccessible() before the update DML so the write respects FLS and silently strips fields the running user cannot edit."), ("D", "Grant Modify All Data to the batch's running user so both the OWD bypass and the FLS restriction are overridden simultaneously by one permission.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "'Without sharing' (A) handles the OWD-driven record-level bypass needed for the read phase, while stripInaccessible() (B) explicitly enforces FLS during the write — each technique independently solves one half of the requirement. A is incorrect: 'With sharing' doesn't automatically strip inaccessible fields during DML. D is incorrect: Modify All Data would bypass FLS too, overcorrecting."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "An architect is reviewing a Visualforce page and a Lightning Web Component that both display the same custom object fields, and the LWC (built with Lightning Data Service via a standard UI API wire adapter) shows different field visibility behavior than the Visualforce page for the same user. What most likely explains this difference?", options: [("A", "FLS only applies to Aura components, not Lightning Web Components, which explains any visibility difference between the two technologies."), ("B", "Lightning Data Service and UI API wire adapters automatically enforce FLS and object permissions, whereas the Visualforce page's custom Apex controller must be explicitly written to respect FLS or it can inadvertently expose fields."), ("C", "The difference must be a platform defect, since Visualforce and LWC are required to always render identical field visibility for the same user."), ("D", "Visualforce pages always enforce FLS more strictly than any Lightning technology, so the LWC is misconfigured and should be rebuilt as Visualforce.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "UI API-backed technologies automatically respect FLS, while a hand-written Visualforce controller must explicitly enforce it. A is incorrect: FLS applies to Aura and LWC alike. C is incorrect: This isn't a platform defect — it's a consequence of implementation choice. D is incorrect: Visualforce doesn't automatically enforce FLS more strictly."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "A large enterprise's security team wants a single object permission that lets a designated 'Data Steward' role fully manage (create, edit, delete) any Contact record in the org without being restricted by OWD, sharing rules, or role hierarchy — but explicitly wants this scoped to Contact only, not every object in the org. Which permission should the architect use?", options: [("A", "View All Data combined with a criteria-based sharing rule matching every possible Contact field value."), ("B", "'Modify All Data' system permission, since it is the only way to bypass OWD and sharing rules for a specific object."), ("C", "'Modify All' object permission on Contact, granted via a permission set, which provides full CRUD authority over every Contact record regardless of sharing while remaining scoped to that one object."), ("D", "Assign the Data Steward role at the very top of the role hierarchy so their access to Contact bypasses OWD automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "The object-level 'Modify All' permission grants full CRUD across every Contact record, scoped to that one object only. A is incorrect: This is unnecessarily convoluted and inconsistent. B is incorrect: Modify All Data applies across all objects, overshooting the requirement. D is incorrect: Role hierarchy position doesn't automatically bypass OWD."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "An architect must ensure that a custom Lightning component enforces the same field-level restrictions as the standard Salesforce UI for a 'Net Worth' field on a Financial_Account__c object, without duplicating FLS logic in Apex for every method that touches the field. Which approach minimizes duplication while guaranteeing enforcement?", options: [("A", "Hard-code a check in each Apex method that compares the running user's profile name against an allow-list of profiles permitted to see Net Worth."), ("B", "Add a custom permission called 'View Net Worth' and check it manually in every Visualforce page but skip Apex entirely since Visualforce enforces FLS automatically."), ("C", "Rely on client-side JavaScript in the Lightning component to hide the Net Worth field from the DOM for unauthorized users."), ("D", "Use Security.stripInaccessible() centrally in a shared utility method called by every Apex method that queries or writes Financial_Account__c, stripping fields the running user cannot access before returning or persisting data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Centralizing stripInaccessible() calls provides consistent, DRY enforcement across every code path. A is incorrect: Hard-coding profile names is brittle and breaks with permission sets or renamed profiles. B is incorrect: Visualforce doesn't automatically enforce custom permissions. C is incorrect: Client-side hiding happens after data is already sent to the browser."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "A logistics company's Shipment_Manager profile needs Create and Edit on Shipment__c but must never delete records once a shipment has been dispatched, even though the profile is shared by dispatchers who occasionally create test records they should be able to remove before dispatch. Which design satisfies both needs?", options: [("A", "Grant Create, Edit, and Delete at the profile level, then use a validation rule that blocks deletion once a 'Dispatched__c' checkbox is set to true, allowing pre-dispatch cleanup while blocking post-dispatch deletion."), ("B", "Grant Modify All on Shipment__c so dispatchers can always manage every record's lifecycle regardless of dispatch status."), ("C", "Grant Create and Edit only, with Delete unchecked entirely, forcing dispatchers to contact an admin whenever a test record needs removal."), ("D", "Grant Delete but remove the delete action from the Lightning page layout only for dispatched records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Combining the Delete permission with a status-scoped validation rule lets dispatchers delete only undispatched records while blocking deletion after dispatch. B is incorrect: Modify All has no relationship to the dispatch-status distinction. C is incorrect: Removing Delete entirely prevents legitimate pre-dispatch cleanup. D is incorrect: Layout-level removal doesn't block API-based deletion."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "A university's admissions office wants an 'Admissions Reviewer' permission set group to inherit Read/Edit on Application__c from one permission set but explicitly lose Edit (retaining Read only) once applications move into a locked 'Decision Finalized' status, using a single group assignment that doesn't change per applicant. Which approach is correct?", options: [("A", "Use a muting permission set to remove Edit only when the org-wide default changes to Private."), ("B", "Keep Edit granted at the permission level for all reviewers, and instead use a record-triggered Flow or validation rule that blocks edits once Decision Finalized is true, since editability by application status is a data-state condition, not a static permission distinction."), ("C", "Rely on FLS to hide the Decision Finalized field so reviewers can't see it changed, indirectly preventing edits."), ("D", "Create two permission set groups and manually reassign every reviewer to the read-only group the moment any application in the org reaches Decision Finalized.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When editability must change based on record state, a validation rule or Flow is correct, leaving the static permission grant unchanged. A is incorrect: Muting isn't triggered by OWD changes; these are unrelated mechanisms. C is incorrect: Hiding the status field doesn't prevent edits to other fields on the record. D is incorrect: Manual reassignment per application status doesn't scale."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A retail bank's teller profile must have Read on Account (banking) records but should never see the 'Credit_Score__c' field regardless of which page layout, list view, or report a teller uses, while loan officers using a different profile need full Read/Edit on that same field. Which configuration achieves this cleanly?", options: [("A", "Apply a validation rule on Account that blocks tellers from saving any record containing a populated Credit_Score__c field."), ("B", "Create a sharing rule that hides the Credit_Score__c value for records owned by tellers."), ("C", "Set Credit_Score__c to Field Hidden in FLS on the teller profile, and Visible/Editable in FLS on the loan officer profile, since FLS is enforced uniformly across every access surface per profile."), ("D", "Remove Credit_Score__c from the teller's page layout only, and add it to the loan officer's page layout, relying on layout assignment as the access boundary.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "FLS is the only mechanism that consistently governs field visibility across every surface, so setting it per-profile guarantees consistent enforcement. A is incorrect: A validation rule fires on save and has nothing to do with read visibility. B is incorrect: Sharing rules control record-level access, not field visibility. D is incorrect: Layout-only changes don't block report columns, list views, or API access."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "An architect is designing permission sets for a consulting firm where 'Junior Consultants' need Create and Read on Project__c but Edit only on projects they created themselves, while 'Senior Consultants' need Edit on any Project__c regardless of creator. What combination of mechanisms correctly implements this distinction?", options: [("A", "Use two different record types, one for Junior-created projects and one for Senior-created projects, since record types alone control who can edit a given record."), ("B", "Grant Junior Consultants Edit only through FLS on the Name field, since FLS is what actually determines whether a user can edit a record they created."), ("C", "Grant Junior Consultants Read only, since object permissions cannot distinguish between records a user created and records they didn't."), ("D", "Grant both roles the Edit object permission via their respective permission sets, then rely on OWD/ownership-based sharing so Junior Consultants only have record-level edit access to projects they own, while Senior Consultants receive broader edit access via a sharing rule or elevated role position.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Object permissions determine maximum capability, but record-level reach for edit is governed by ownership-based sharing, naturally limiting Junior Consultants to owned records. A is incorrect: Record types govern layouts/picklists, not record-level edit access. B is incorrect: FLS has no bearing on the ownership-based distinction. C is incorrect: Object permissions plus OWD absolutely can produce this distinction."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "A media company wants contractors accessing Salesforce via a Customer Community Plus license to have Create and Edit on a custom 'Content_Submission__c' object, but internal employees using standard licenses should retain full CRUD. The architect must verify whether the contractor's license even supports the needed object permissions before designing sharing. What should the architect check first?", options: [("A", "Confirm whether the license type assigned to the contractor's profile supports object permissions and custom object access at all, since some license types restrict which objects and permissions can be granted regardless of profile configuration."), ("B", "Assume all Salesforce license types support identical object permission configurations, since object permissions are licensed independently of user license type."), ("C", "Skip license verification and configure FLS first, since FLS access is unrelated to license type."), ("D", "Check only the permission set license, since base user license never affects object permission availability.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Different license types have different ceilings on which objects/permissions they can be granted; no permission set can grant access beyond what the base license allows. B is incorrect: License type is a hard constraint on available object permissions. C is incorrect: FLS is subject to the same license ceiling as object permissions. D is incorrect: Base user license independently constrains object-level access alongside permission set licenses."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "A pharmaceutical company's Quality Assurance profile must be able to view every field on a 'Batch_Record__c' object including ones normally hidden from other profiles, without granting QA the ability to edit any field. Which FLS configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "Grant QA the Edit object permission but hide the Save button from their page layout so they cannot actually submit changes."), ("B", "Set every field on Batch_Record__c to 'Visible' but not 'Editable' in the QA profile's field-level security, giving full read transparency while structurally preventing any field-level write."), ("C", "Set fields to 'Editable' without 'Visible' in FLS, since editable fields are automatically also visible with read-only enforcement handled elsewhere."), ("D", "Grant View All Data to QA, since it automatically sets every field's FLS to visible-only across the org for the requesting user.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Visible controls read access while Editable controls write access; setting Visible without Editable structurally blocks any write attempt regardless of access path. A is incorrect: Hiding a UI button doesn't block API-based writes. C is incorrect: FLS requires Visible to be checked for a field to be readable; this configuration is invalid. D is incorrect: View All Data has no effect on FLS settings."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A gaming company's support agents need to update the 'Resolution_Notes__c' field on Case, but a new compliance requirement says agents must never be able to modify the 'Root_Cause__c' field once populated by a specialist team, even though agents can still see it. Which configuration satisfies this?", options: [("A", "Use a sharing rule that restricts Root_Cause__c edits to the specialist team's role only."), ("B", "Set the entire Case object to Read-only for agents, since object-level Read prevents editing any single field while still allowing Resolution_Notes__c updates through a separate integration."), ("C", "Set Root_Cause__c to Visible but not Editable in FLS on the agent profile, while Resolution_Notes__c remains both Visible and Editable, achieving field-specific read/write control that doesn't affect the rest of the object."), ("D", "Add Root_Cause__c to a separate object entirely so agents structurally cannot access it via the Case layout.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "FLS operates per field independently, achieving the mixed read/write requirement on a single object without restructuring. A is incorrect: Sharing rules operate at the record level, not the field level. B is incorrect: Object-wide Read-only would also block the required Resolution_Notes__c edits. D is incorrect: Splitting the field onto a different object is unnecessary and breaks the requirement that agents still view it on Case."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "An architect needs to grant a 'Regional Auditor' permission set the ability to view all Contract__c records org-wide for compliance audits, but the object already has several existing criteria-based sharing rules granting broader access to other groups. The auditor's manager insists auditors must never be able to edit records, even accidentally through automation triggered by a view action. Which TWO properties of the 'View All' object permission make it the safe choice here?", options: [("A", "It is scoped only to Contract__c when configured through a permission set rather than a profile, unlike View All Data."), ("B", "It only affects the auditor's own read access and has no interaction with existing sharing rules or other users' access."), ("C", "It aggregates visibility across every public group referenced in existing sharing rules automatically."), ("D", "It does not grant any write capability, so there is no risk of accidental edits.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "View All is additive and read-only (A), granting visibility into every record without altering existing sharing rules, and it grants no write capability (B), so there is no risk of accidental edits even through automation. * C: View All (object-level) is inherently scoped to one object by nature of being an object permission; this isn't a distinguishing property versus View All Data in the way stated — View All Data is always system-wide regardless of assignment method. * D: View All does not automatically aggregate group memberships; that's not how the permission works."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "An architect is troubleshooting why a 'Partner Success Manager' can edit Opportunity Amount on records but a change to a different currency-related field silently fails via the API with no error surfaced to the calling integration, even though the same user can edit it fine in the UI. What is the most likely FLS-related explanation?", options: [("A", "The Partner Success Manager's own FLS must have changed automatically overnight due to currency exchange rate updates."), ("B", "FLS is applied only in Lightning Experience and never in API calls, so this must be an unrelated bug in the integration code."), ("C", "Currency fields are exempt from FLS by design and always allow API writes regardless of profile configuration."), ("D", "The integration uses a different running user (an integration/service account) than the Partner Success Manager, and that running user's profile has the currency field set to non-editable in FLS, so the API silently ignores or rejects the field update for that context.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "FLS is evaluated per running user, and an integration account's own FLS can restrict a field even though a different human user's FLS permits it. A is incorrect: There is no field-type-based exemption from FLS. B is incorrect: FLS settings don't change automatically based on exchange rate updates. D is incorrect: FLS is enforced for both UI and API access under standard execution contexts."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "An architect must design permissions so that a 'Contract Reviewer' permission set grants Read on Contract__c and its child Contract_Line_Item__c, but reviewers should never be able to see the 'Internal_Margin__c' field on the child object, even though they need to see every other child field for review purposes. Which TWO configuration steps, together, achieve this?", options: [("A", "Grant object-level Read only on Contract__c, since child object permissions are automatically inherited and restricted through the Master-Detail relationship without separate configuration."), ("B", "Set the entire Contract_Line_Item__c object to Field Hidden, since object-level hiding is required before individual field-level exceptions can be layered on top."), ("C", "Grant object-level Read on both Contract__c and Contract_Line_Item__c."), ("D", "Set Internal_Margin__c to Field Hidden in FLS specifically, leaving all other child fields Visible.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Object permissions and FLS are independent per object; granting Read on both objects (A) and hiding just the sensitive field via FLS (B) together achieve precise restriction while preserving every other child field. A is incorrect: Object permissions are not automatically inherited across a Master-Detail relationship. B is incorrect: There's no such prerequisite; individual FLS per field doesn't require first hiding the entire object."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "An HR system integration writes to a 'Salary__c' field on a custom Employee__c object using a dedicated integration user. The security team wants assurance that even if the integration is compromised, the integration user cannot read or write to any object besides Employee__c and its immediate related objects. What should the architect verify?", options: [("A", "That the integration user's profile and any assigned permission sets grant object permissions narrowly scoped to only Employee__c and the specific related objects required, with no broad permissions like View All Data or Modify All Data assigned anywhere."), ("B", "That the integration user has Modify All Data, since broad access is required for any integration to function reliably regardless of scope."), ("C", "That FLS alone restricts the integration user, since object permissions do not matter once FLS is configured correctly."), ("D", "That the integration uses OAuth, since OAuth authentication alone limits which objects an API call can touch regardless of the underlying user's permissions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The principle of least privilege requires auditing the integration user's actual object permissions to confirm no broad, org-wide grants exist. B is incorrect: Modify All Data is the opposite of least-privilege design. C is incorrect: FLS has no bearing on which objects are accessible in the first place. D is incorrect: OAuth governs authentication scopes, but the underlying user's Salesforce permissions still determine data access."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "An architect is finalizing the security model for 'Case Escalation Specialists' who need Edit on Case but must be prevented from ever changing the 'Case Owner' field to reassign cases away from the queue-based routing system, while still being able to edit every other Case field freely. Which configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "Set OwnerId to Field Hidden in FLS for the Escalation Specialist profile, since OwnerId behaves identically to any custom lookup field under FLS."), ("B", "Since Case Owner is a special lookup-like field governed by ownership/reassignment mechanics rather than standard FLS, use a validation rule or Flow that blocks owner changes by this profile, since FLS cannot target the OwnerId field the way it targets custom fields."), ("C", "Remove Edit entirely from the Case object, since Edit and Delete are the only permissions relevant to preventing ownership changes."), ("D", "Grant View All on Case instead of Edit, since View All structurally removes the ability to change ownership while preserving all other edit capabilities.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "OwnerId is a special system field not fully governed by standard FLS, so preventing reassignment while preserving all other Edit capability requires a validation rule or Flow targeting ownership changes specifically. A is incorrect: OwnerId does not behave identically to a standard custom lookup under FLS. C is incorrect: Removing Edit entirely blocks all the other field edits specialists need. D is incorrect: View All is read-only and would remove Edit capability altogether."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "A logistics company sets Organization-Wide Default for Shipment__c to Public Read/Write to simplify onboarding, then later asks the architect why none of the 40 sharing rules built for regional teams appear to have any effect. What should the architect explain?", options: [("A", "OWD only affects the UI, so the sharing rules are still fully effective for API and integration access."), ("B", "Sharing rules always take precedence over OWD, so the 40 rules should still be restricting access below the Public Read/Write baseline as designed."), ("C", "Public Read/Write already grants every user full read/write access baseline-wide, so any sharing rule that only grants Read or Read/Write access is redundant since it cannot expand access beyond what OWD already provides."), ("D", "Sharing rules require a role hierarchy to function, and the issue is unrelated to OWD.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "OWD sets the baseline record access; sharing rules can only widen access beyond that baseline, never restrict it, so when OWD is already the maximum, rules become functionally inert. A is incorrect: OWD governs record access uniformly across UI, API, and reports. B is incorrect: Sharing mechanisms are strictly additive and cannot narrow access below OWD. D is incorrect: Sharing rules don't require a role hierarchy; this doesn't explain the redundancy."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A staffing agency has two personas: 'Branch Managers' who must see every candidate record submitted by their direct and indirect reports, and 'Recruiters' who should only see their own submitted candidates by default. Given a strict Private OWD on Candidate__c, which TWO steps, together, achieve this with the least additional configuration?", options: [("A", "Place Branch Managers above Recruiters in the role hierarchy."), ("B", "Ensure 'Grant Access Using Hierarchies' is enabled for Candidate__c, so managers automatically inherit access to all subordinate-owned records."), ("C", "Create a criteria-based sharing rule matching every possible candidate status value to simulate hierarchy-based access for managers."), ("D", "Manually share every Candidate__c record from each recruiter to their manager whenever a new candidate is created.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Placing managers above recruiters in the hierarchy (A) combined with 'Grant Access Using Hierarchies' enabled (B) automatically gives managers read/write access matching the owner's access level — both steps are needed together. C is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules match field values, not organizational position, and don't scale. D is incorrect: Manual sharing per record is an operational burden with no automation."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "An organization's VP of Sales reports directly to the CRO in the role hierarchy, but the VP does not automatically see Opportunities owned by a Solutions Engineer who reports to a completely separate Engineering VP, even though both eventually roll up to the CEO. What explains this behavior?", options: [("A", "Grant Access Using Hierarchies only applies to Opportunities owned by users in the same profile as the viewer."), ("B", "Role hierarchy access is granted to every user in the org regardless of hierarchy position, so this must be caused by a misconfigured sharing rule blocking the VP."), ("C", "The CEO's position at the top of the hierarchy blocks downward propagation of access to any VP below them."), ("D", "Role hierarchy grants implicit access strictly along the reporting line above a record's owner; since the Solutions Engineer's chain doesn't pass through the Sales VP, the Sales VP is not 'above' that owner in the hierarchy and gains no automatic access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Hierarchy-based implicit access flows strictly upward along a record owner's own management chain; a parallel branch is not a hierarchical superior of the owner. A is incorrect: Grant Access Using Hierarchies is based on hierarchical position, not matching profiles. B is incorrect: Role hierarchy access requires a superior-subordinate relationship relative to the record owner. C is incorrect: Hierarchy access simply doesn't flow sideways between parallel branches."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "A conglomerate uses a very deep, 12-level role hierarchy mirroring its full corporate org chart, and the architect notices sharing recalculations after bulk data loads regularly take hours and sometimes time out. What should the architect recommend to address the pattern while preserving manager-based visibility?", options: [("A", "Flatten the role hierarchy to fewer levels focused only on data-access needs (rather than mirroring the entire corporate structure) since fewer levels reduce the sharing recalculation overhead while still preserving the necessary manager-to-subordinate visibility."), ("B", "Add more roles to the hierarchy to spread the load across additional recalculation batches, since more granular roles always improve recalculation performance."), ("C", "Disable Grant Access Using Hierarchies entirely, since that setting is unrelated to recalculation time and only affects UI rendering speed."), ("D", "Switch every object's OWD to Public Read/Write to eliminate the need for hierarchy-based access recalculation entirely, since it has no known trade-offs at scale.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Deep role hierarchies mirroring an entire org chart are a known scalability anti-pattern; flattening to only access-relevant levels reduces recalculation overhead while preserving manager visibility. B is incorrect: Adding more roles increases recalculation complexity, contradicting the goal. C is incorrect: Disabling Grant Access Using Hierarchies removes manager visibility entirely and does affect recalculation. D is incorrect: Public Read/Write eliminates all record-level restriction, a major trade-off this option ignores."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A conglomerate's business units operate as a matrix, where an engineer reports functionally to a Technical Director but administratively to a Regional Manager, and both need visibility into the engineer's Project__c records without either being the engineer's sole role-hierarchy parent. Which approach best compensates for the single-parent limitation of role hierarchy?", options: [("A", "Restructure the role hierarchy so the engineer reports to both managers simultaneously, since Salesforce roles natively support multiple parents."), ("B", "Use a public group or a team-based sharing mechanism (or an additional sharing rule) that explicitly grants the second manager access, since role hierarchy alone only supports a single reporting line per user and cannot represent a true matrix."), ("C", "Rely entirely on OWD set to Public Read Only, since that automatically satisfies any matrix-reporting visibility requirement without extra configuration."), ("D", "Assign the engineer two separate user records, one per reporting line, so each manager's hierarchy naturally includes one of the records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Since each role supports only a single parent, matrix structures require a supplementary mechanism like a sharing rule targeting a public group to grant the second manager visibility. A is incorrect: Salesforce roles are single-parent by design. C is incorrect: Public Read Only exposes all records to everyone, far broader than needed. D is incorrect: Duplicate user records violate licensing terms and break ownership integrity."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "A B2B software company needs all Cases tagged with record type 'Enterprise Escalation' to be visible to a dedicated Tier 2 support group, regardless of who owns each Case, while Cases of other record types remain governed by standard ownership-based sharing. Which sharing rule design is correct?", options: [("A", "Change the OWD for Case to Public Read/Write specifically for the Enterprise Escalation record type, since OWD can be scoped per record type."), ("B", "Create an ownership-based sharing rule that shares all Cases owned by the Tier 2 group's manager, since ownership-based rules can filter by record type natively."), ("C", "Create a criteria-based sharing rule filtering on RecordType equals Enterprise Escalation, sharing matching records with the Tier 2 public group at the desired access level."), ("D", "Add the Tier 2 group as members of a queue and assign all Cases regardless of type to that queue.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Criteria-based sharing rules share records meeting specific field criteria like record type with a target group, without touching ownership. A is incorrect: OWD is set per object, not per record type. B is incorrect: Ownership-based rules key off owner/role, not record type criteria. D is incorrect: Reassigning matching Cases into a queue would change actual ownership, disrupting other Cases' standard sharing."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "A retailer's customer service queue holds unassigned Cases, and management wants every queue member to be able to edit any Case sitting in that queue, while non-members must have no access at all to those unassigned records. Which native mechanism satisfies this directly?", options: [("A", "A manual share created individually for every Case as it enters the queue."), ("B", "A criteria-based sharing rule matching Case Owner equals each individual queue member's user ID, updated manually whenever membership changes."), ("C", "Public Read/Write OWD on Case, since it is required for any queue-based visibility to function at all."), ("D", "Queue membership itself, since records assigned to a queue are accessible to all queue members at the access level defined for queue-based visibility, without needing a separate sharing rule.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "When a record's owner is a queue, all members automatically gain access, directly satisfying the requirement without additional configuration. A is incorrect: Manual sharing per record is unscalable when queue membership provides the same access automatically. B is incorrect: Manually maintaining owner-matching rules per member doesn't scale. C is incorrect: Queue-based access works under Private OWD; Public Read/Write would defeat the purpose."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "An insurance company built a criteria-based sharing rule on Claim__c that shares records where 'Claim_Status__c' equals 'Under Review' with the Legal team. Adjusters report that when they change a claim's status away from 'Under Review', the Legal team still retains access for several minutes afterward. What explains this, and is it a design flaw?", options: [("A", "Criteria-based sharing rules recalculate asynchronously in the background whenever a matching field changes, so there is a brief window where cached sharing entries haven't yet been removed — this is expected platform behavior, not a flaw requiring urgent architectural change."), ("B", "This indicates a permanent bug where criteria-based sharing rules never remove access once granted, and the only fix is manual share deletion after every status change."), ("C", "Criteria-based sharing rules only apply to newly created records, so any existing claim keeps its original sharing forever regardless of status changes."), ("D", "The Legal team must have View All Data assigned, since criteria-based sharing rules cannot expire access under any configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Sharing recalculation happens asynchronously, so there can be a delay between a field change and the share entries updating — expected, not a defect. B is incorrect: Access is removed once recalculation completes; it is not permanent. C is incorrect: Criteria-based rules re-evaluate on relevant field changes for existing records too. D is incorrect: View All Data is unrelated to this recalculation lag."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "A large enterprise wants to keep record access flexible using role-based sharing rules but is concerned about hitting Salesforce's sharing rule limits as more business units are onboarded onto Case. Which TWO statements about the sharing rule limits per object are correct?", options: [("A", "Owner-based sharing rules are capped at 300 per object."), ("B", "There is no limit on sharing rules per object as long as guest users are excluded from all of them."), ("C", "Criteria-based sharing rules are capped at 300 per object, separate from the owner-based cap, for a combined ceiling of 600."), ("D", "Owner-based and criteria-based rules share a single unified counter capped at 300 total per object.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Salesforce enforces separate limits of 300 owner-based (A) and 300 criteria-based (B) sharing rules per object, independently of each other, for a combined ceiling of 600 rules on a single object. B is incorrect: There is a hard platform limit regardless of guest user involvement. D is incorrect: Owner-based and criteria-based rules have separate 300-rule limits, not one shared counter."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "A nonprofit's data team wants to share Volunteer_Shift__c records with a cross-functional committee spanning members from three different roles and two different profiles, none of which share a common hierarchy branch. What group type is most maintainable for this sharing target?", options: [("A", "A role, since committee members can simply all be reassigned into one new shared role for this purpose without disrupting their existing reporting lines."), ("B", "A public group containing the specific individual users (or nested roles) that make up the committee, referenced by a sharing rule, since public groups can aggregate members across roles and profiles regardless of hierarchy position."), ("C", "A queue, since queues are the correct mechanism whenever a cross-functional team needs shared visibility into records they don't own."), ("D", "The role hierarchy alone, since 'Roles and Subordinates' sharing rule targets can represent any arbitrary cross-functional group without needing a public group.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Public groups aggregate arbitrary combinations of users/roles/other groups regardless of hierarchy position, making them the most maintainable target here. A is incorrect: Reassigning real roles just for sharing disrupts actual reporting-based access elsewhere. C is incorrect: Queues are designed around actual record ownership/work distribution, not general committee sharing. D is incorrect: 'Roles and Subordinates' still requires a coherent hierarchy branch."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "An architect nests twelve public groups inside one another to represent a complex cross-departmental sharing structure for Account, and now sharing rule recalculation after mass Account imports takes significantly longer than before the nesting was introduced. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Convert every nested group into a queue, since queues never have any recalculation overhead regardless of structure."), ("B", "Add even more nested groups to further subdivide membership, since additional nesting levels always improve recalculation performance by parallelizing group evaluation."), ("C", "Flatten the nested group structure into fewer, more direct group memberships since deeply nested public groups increase the computational complexity of sharing rule evaluation and recalculation at scale."), ("D", "Disable sharing rule recalculation entirely for Account, since nested groups are incompatible with any form of recalculation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Deeply nested public groups increase the resolution complexity for effective membership during recalculation; flattening reduces that overhead. A is incorrect: Queues serve a different purpose and aren't a drop-in replacement for nested public group sharing. B is incorrect: More nesting increases, not decreases, complexity. D is incorrect: Recalculation can't simply be disabled for an object; doing so would break sharing rule-based access entirely."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "A financial firm's Opportunity Teams are used so account executives can pull in specialists with custom access levels per deal, but the compliance team asks whether Opportunity Teams can also grant those same specialists access to the related Account's custom child object 'Deal_Risk_Assessment__c'. What should the architect explain?", options: [("A", "Deal_Risk_Assessment__c will always inherit Opportunity Team access automatically as long as it uses a Lookup relationship to Opportunity."), ("B", "Opportunity Teams automatically cascade access to every custom object anywhere in the org related to the Opportunity's Account, including Deal_Risk_Assessment__c, with no further configuration needed."), ("C", "Opportunity Teams can never grant access to anything beyond the Opportunity's own fields, not even related Account access."), ("D", "Opportunity Teams grant access only to the Opportunity record itself (and optionally related records through team-related sharing settings for Account), not automatically to arbitrary custom child objects like Deal_Risk_Assessment__c, which would require separate sharing configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Opportunity Teams are scoped to the Opportunity (and can extend limited Account access under certain configurations), but don't automatically propagate to unrelated custom child objects. A is incorrect: A Lookup relationship doesn't automatically inherit Opportunity Team sharing. B is incorrect: This overstates reach — cascading to every custom object isn't how the feature works. C is incorrect: Opportunity Teams can extend limited Account access under certain configurations."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "A services company's Case Teams and Account Teams are both in use, and a support lead asks the architect why adding someone to a Case Team required specifying their access level individually per Case, while Account Team access seemed to follow a predefined template. What is the correct explanation?", options: [("A", "Account Teams support default team templates that predefine roles and access levels applied automatically when the template is used, whereas Case Team membership is typically configured per Case (or via Case Team templates/predefined teams) with access levels set individually unless a similar predefined structure is established."), ("B", "Case Teams and Account Teams are functionally identical in every respect, so any observed difference must be a user error in configuration."), ("C", "Only Account Teams exist natively; Case Teams are a deprecated legacy feature no longer available in current orgs."), ("D", "Case Teams always inherit the exact access levels defined on the related Account Team automatically, with no independent configuration possible.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Account Team default templates let administrators predefine a standard set of roles and access levels applied consistently, while Case Team access, unless similarly templated, is more commonly configured per Case, per member. B is incorrect: The two features have real configurational differences, so claiming total functional identity is incorrect. C is incorrect: Case Teams are an actively supported feature, not deprecated. D is incorrect: Case Team access levels are not automatically derived from a related Account Team's settings."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "A sales rep adds a solutions engineer to an Opportunity Team with 'Read Only' access, but the solutions engineer needs to update a technical requirements field directly on the Opportunity before the deal closes. What additional step is required?", options: [("A", "Nothing further is needed, since Opportunity Team members always receive Read/Write access after 30 days automatically regardless of the initially selected level."), ("B", "The sales rep (or an admin) must update the Opportunity Team member's access level to Read/Write, since the access level assigned when adding the member directly determines their record-level capability on that Opportunity."), ("C", "The solutions engineer must be granted Modify All Data, since Opportunity Team access levels cannot be changed once initially set."), ("D", "The sales rep must remove and recreate the entire Opportunity Team from scratch, since access levels cannot be edited for existing members.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Opportunity Team member access level is editable; changing it from Read Only to Read/Write directly grants the additional edit capability needed. A is incorrect: There's no automatic escalation of access levels over time. C is incorrect: Modify All Data is far broader than needed, and team access levels can be edited. D is incorrect: Access level changes don't require deleting and recreating the whole team."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "A subscription company uses a Master-Detail relationship from Invoice_Line__c to Invoice__c, and a Lookup relationship from Invoice__c to Account. A billing specialist has Read access to a specific Account via a sharing rule but no direct access to the Invoice__c records under it. What access, if any, does the specialist have to the Invoice_Line__c records under an Invoice they cannot see?", options: [("A", "Read-only access, since Lookup relationships to Account automatically grant read access to all Master-Detail descendants regardless of intermediate object access."), ("B", "Full access, since Master-Detail relationships always cascade access transitively across every relationship type in the chain, including Lookup relationships further up."), ("C", "None automatically — Master-Detail sharing cascades from Invoice__c to Invoice_Line__c, but since the specialist has no access to the parent Invoice__c itself (Account-to-Invoice being only a Lookup, not Master-Detail), no cascading access reaches the Invoice_Line__c records either."), ("D", "Full access, since Account-level sharing rules always override Master-Detail cascading rules for any descendant object.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Master-Detail cascading only propagates from a directly accessible parent to its children; since Account-to-Invoice is a Lookup, Account access doesn't cascade to Invoice__c, so the Invoice_Line__c cascade has no accessible parent to originate from. A is incorrect: Lookup relationships don't cascade record access the way Master-Detail does. B is incorrect: Cascading requires each link to itself be Master-Detail with an accessible parent. D is incorrect: There's no such override rule for grandparent sharing rules."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "A B2C retailer's Customer Community license is being replaced with Customer Community Plus because portal users need to see records owned by other portal users within the same account hierarchy under a role structure. Which TWO capabilities does Customer Community Plus provide that base Customer Community lacks?", options: [("A", "A role hierarchy for external users."), ("B", "Removal of the need for OWD configuration entirely for external objects."), ("C", "Full role hierarchy identical to internal users, which base Customer Community already supports."), ("D", "Standard role-based sharing rules for external users.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Customer Community Plus supports a role hierarchy for external users (A) plus standard role-based sharing rules (B), capabilities base Customer Community lacks, exactly matching the required gap. B is incorrect: External OWD is still required regardless of Experience Cloud license. C is incorrect: Base Customer Community explicitly lacks role hierarchy for external users."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "A partner portal built on Customer Community Plus needs certain designated partner users to see all records owned by any user within their own partner account, not just their own records, without being made a super user. Which mechanism achieves this at the account level?", options: [("A", "Role hierarchy alone, without any account-hierarchy consideration, governs all portal user cross-visibility."), ("B", "Only super user access can provide account-level visibility across other portal users' records; there is no other mechanism."), ("C", "External OWD must be set to Public Read/Write for the object to achieve this, which also grants that access to every unrelated portal account."), ("D", "Account hierarchy-based implicit sharing, where a portal user gains access to records owned by other users at their own account (and any child accounts in the account hierarchy), independent of super user status.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Account hierarchy-based implicit sharing automatically extends visibility to records owned by other users at the same account (and its hierarchy), achieving the requirement without super user escalation. A is incorrect: Portal role hierarchy works in conjunction with account hierarchy for this pattern. B is incorrect: Implicit account-hierarchy sharing already provides this without super user designation. C is incorrect: Public Read/Write would expose records to every portal account broadly."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "A distributor's partner portal has 'super user' access enabled for select partner account administrators. What does super user access specifically extend, and when is it the right call?", options: [("A", "Super user access extends a portal user's visibility to records owned by other users within the same account hierarchy at a broader level (beyond default implicit sharing), and is appropriate when specific partner administrators need this fuller cross-user visibility within their account's hierarchy for legitimate management purposes."), ("B", "Super user access grants full internal View All Data permission automatically to any portal user who receives it."), ("C", "Super user access is only relevant for Customer Community (not Plus) and has no effect under Customer Community Plus."), ("D", "Super user access should be granted to every portal user by default since it carries no additional risk beyond standard implicit sharing.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Super user access broadens a portal user's visibility across their account hierarchy beyond default implicit sharing, appropriate specifically for users like partner administrators who legitimately need broader oversight. B is incorrect: Super user access doesn't grant internal-level View All Data. C is incorrect: Super user access is relevant to both Customer Community and Plus contexts. D is incorrect: Granting it broadly increases exposure and should be limited to those who need it."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "A telecom's customer community should let each customer see only their own Cases, plus any Case a support agent has explicitly decided to share with them for transparency on a joint troubleshooting effort. Which combination is correct?", options: [("A", "Set external OWD for Case to Public Read/Write, then rely on customers self-restricting which Cases they choose to view."), ("B", "Set external OWD for Case to Private, and have agents use manual sharing to grant the customer access to specific Cases requiring joint visibility, layering an exception on top of the default private baseline."), ("C", "Use a criteria-based sharing rule that matches every Case, since criteria-based rules are the only way to share individual records with external users."), ("D", "Rely solely on account hierarchy-based implicit sharing, since it automatically limits customers to only their own Cases with no further configuration needed for exceptions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A Private external OWD baseline ensures customers only see their own Cases by default, and manual sharing provides the exact one-off exception for joint visibility. A is incorrect: Public Read/Write exposes every Case to every customer. C is incorrect: Criteria-based rules aren't designed for one-off, discretionary agent decisions. D is incorrect: Implicit sharing doesn't provide the discretionary exception needed."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "A public-facing support site uses Guest User access to let unauthenticated visitors submit and view their own support tickets by a reference number, with no login required. The security team asks whether guest users can be added to a public group to grant them elevated access consistent with authenticated customers. Which TWO platform restrictions apply to guest users here?", options: [("A", "Guest users automatically inherit View All Data since they have no role and therefore no baseline restriction applies."), ("B", "Guest users have no user role, so role hierarchy-based sharing cannot be used for them."), ("C", "Guest users cannot receive manual shares, as a security safeguard for unauthenticated access."), ("D", "Guest user access is identical to Customer Community Plus access once Experience Cloud is enabled.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Guest users have no role (A) and cannot receive manual shares (B) — both are platform restrictions applied because they're unauthenticated, so architects must rely on compensating mechanisms like criteria-based sharing rules. A is incorrect: Lacking a role doesn't grant elevated default access. D is incorrect: Guest user access is a distinct, more restricted model."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "A regulated utility company wants to compensate for guest user sharing limitations while still letting unauthenticated visitors see specific outage-related Case records matching certain criteria (e.g., a public tracking status) without exposing all Cases. What is the correct compensating pattern?", options: [("A", "Add the guest user to a standard public group referenced by an ownership-based sharing rule, since guest users work identically to internal users for group membership."), ("B", "Grant the guest user profile Modify All Data on Case, since that is the simplest way to guarantee the specific outage Cases are visible."), ("C", "Configure a tightly scoped external OWD with a criteria-based sharing rule matching the specific public-safe criteria to expose only those matching records to the guest user context, since guest users can be covered by criteria-based sharing rules even though they can't receive manual shares."), ("D", "Rely on manual sharing performed by a support agent for each outage Case as it's created, since that is the primary supported mechanism for guest user record exposure.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Criteria-based sharing rules remain viable and supported for surfacing specific matching records to the guest user's restricted context. A is incorrect: Guest users have documented restrictions around group-based sharing patterns. B is incorrect: Modify All Data is a sweeping, inappropriate grant for an unauthenticated context. D is incorrect: Manual sharing is specifically unavailable to guest users."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "A wealth management firm's compliance officer asks the architect to explain why, despite Contact having a Private OWD, a specific Compliance Officer role can see every Contact in the org without any sharing rule referencing that role. Which TWO permissions, if assigned to that role's profile or a permission set, would independently explain this behavior?", options: [("A", "Placement at the very top of the role hierarchy with no 'Grant Access Using Hierarchies' setting enabled."), ("B", "'View All Data', a system-wide permission that overrides OWD, sharing rules, and role hierarchy for every object, including Contact."), ("C", "A criteria-based sharing rule that was deactivated more than 90 days ago but technically remains defined in Setup."), ("D", "'View All' on Contact specifically, an object-level permission that overrides OWD, sharing rules, and role hierarchy for that object only.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Either 'View All Data' (A, org-wide) or the narrower object-level 'View All' on Contact (B) independently bypasses OWD, sharing rules, and hierarchy entirely, making either one a valid explanation the architect should check for. A is incorrect: Hierarchy access requires 'Grant Access Using Hierarchies' and only grants access to subordinates' records, not blanket visibility, even from the top of the hierarchy. C is incorrect: A deactivated sharing rule has no effect on current access regardless of how long ago it was deactivated."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "An architect is asked whether a System Administrator profile can be restricted from seeing certain highly sensitive records even though administrators traditionally see everything. What is the architecturally correct answer?", options: [("A", "Sharing rules can be configured specifically to exclude System Administrator profiles from their scope, which is the standard way to restrict admin visibility."), ("B", "System Administrators can never be restricted under any circumstances since the platform hard-codes unrestricted access for any profile literally named System Administrator."), ("C", "Removing 'View All Data' alone is always sufficient to fully restrict a System Administrator profile from every object, since it is the only permission administrators rely on."), ("D", "By default, profiles with 'Modify All Data' (typically System Administrator) bypass all sharing restrictions, so truly restricting admins requires either not granting that permission on a custom admin-like profile or exploring compensating controls, since 'Modify All Data' inherently overrides sharing for its scope.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The unrestricted visibility commonly associated with admins stems from the 'Modify All Data' system permission, not a hard-coded rule about the profile's name; genuinely restricting such access requires a custom profile without that permission or other compensating design. A is incorrect: Sharing rules can't exclude specific profiles this way, and Modify All Data bypasses sharing regardless of any rule. B is incorrect: Behavior stems from assigned permissions, not the literal profile name. C is incorrect: Admins typically carry both View All Data and Modify All Data; removing only one still leaves the other capable of overriding sharing."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "A support manager is confused why they can see a specific Case that doesn't appear to be shared through any sharing rule they're aware of, and asks the architect to diagnose it without changing any configuration. Which TWO native tools can independently surface the source of that access?", options: [("A", "The Setup Audit Trail, since it lists every sharing reason applied to any record in the org in a single searchable log."), ("B", "The user's Login History, since it shows a breakdown of which sharing sources granted access during that specific login session."), ("C", "The 'Why can I see this record?' Record Access / Sharing Hierarchy view for the specific record, which surfaces every source contributing to that user's access — OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual shares, teams, or Apex shares."), ("D", "The Sharing Debugger, a separate tool that provides a similar targeted breakdown of access sources for a given user and record.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Both the Record Access / Sharing Hierarchy view (A) and the Sharing Debugger (B) are purpose-built, independent tools that each enumerate the specific access sources contributing to a user's visibility into a specific record. * C: Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not live per-record access attribution. * D: Login History has no visibility into per-record sharing sources."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "Using High Volume Customer Portal (HVCP) users for a massive self-service claims portal, the architect notes several standard sharing mechanisms won't apply. Which of the following is unavailable for HVCP users, and what typically replaces it?", options: [("A", "HVCP users do not have roles and cannot use role hierarchy-based sharing; access is typically handled instead through account/contact-based implicit sharing patterns and sharing sets rather than role hierarchy."), ("B", "HVCP users support full role hierarchy identical to Customer Community Plus, so no compensating mechanism is needed."), ("C", "HVCP users cannot be assigned to any object at all, making the entire portal architecture infeasible regardless of design."), ("D", "HVCP users automatically receive Modify All Data by default to compensate for the lack of role hierarchy.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "HVCP users lack individual roles, so role hierarchy-based sharing is unavailable; sharing sets and account/contact-based implicit sharing compensate. A is incorrect: HVCP users can be granted access through sharing sets and other supported mechanisms. B is incorrect: There's no default grant of Modify All Data to HVCP users. D is incorrect: HVCP explicitly lacks the role hierarchy support Customer Community Plus has."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "An executive dashboard built on Opportunity reports must always show every Opportunity regardless of which executive views it, even though Opportunity has a Private OWD and viewers have wildly different sharing access. What configuration achieves this?", options: [("A", "Set the dashboard to 'Run as viewer' and grant View All to every executive individually, since running-user mode has no effect on dashboard consistency."), ("B", "Set the dashboard's running user to a designated user (e.g., an admin or integration user) with View All on Opportunity, so every viewer sees the same complete dataset regardless of their own individual access."), ("C", "Change Opportunity's OWD to Public Read Only, since dashboards cannot use a fixed running user under any configuration."), ("D", "Share the underlying report folder with every executive at Manager access, since folder access alone guarantees dashboard data completeness.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Setting a dashboard's running user to a specific user with View All makes every viewer see the same, complete dataset regardless of their own sharing access. A is incorrect: 'Run as viewer' ties results to each individual viewer's own access, the opposite of the consistency goal. C is incorrect: Public Read Only exposes every Opportunity broadly, a far larger change than needed, and dashboards do support a fixed running user. D is incorrect: Folder access doesn't override underlying record-level sharing."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "A security team flags that a company's revenue dashboard has its running user set to a System Administrator with View All Data, and asks the architect whether this configuration poses a risk. What's the correct assessment?", options: [("A", "No — dashboard running users never affect what data is queried, only how charts are visually rendered."), ("B", "No — View All Data only affects Setup access, not report or dashboard data, so there's no meaningful exposure risk from this configuration."), ("C", "Yes — using a broad system-wide permission like View All Data as the running user exposes every object's data through the dashboard's underlying reports, not just the intended Opportunity data, so a narrower running user with only the object-specific View All permission needed is a safer choice."), ("D", "No — because dashboards automatically strip out any fields the intended audience shouldn't see, regardless of the running user's actual permissions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "View All Data spans every object, so using it as a running user means any report on any object reflects that unrestricted access; an object-scoped View All is safer. A is incorrect: The running user's access directly determines which records are queried. B is incorrect: View All Data absolutely affects reporting/data access, not just Setup. D is incorrect: Dashboards don't automatically strip fields based on audience."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "A finance team wants a specific 'Executive Reporting' folder shared so a group of VPs can run any report inside it, but those VPs should still only see the underlying records they're individually permitted to see per standard sharing — the folder is just for organizing reports, not expanding visibility. What should the architect confirm about folder sharing?", options: [("A", "Report folders cannot be shared with groups; only individual users can be granted folder access."), ("B", "Sharing a report folder automatically grants View All Data to every member for every object referenced by reports in that folder."), ("C", "Folder sharing and record-level sharing are the same mechanism, so granting folder access necessarily expands what records members can see."), ("D", "Report folder sharing controls who can access/run/edit the reports stored in the folder, but it does not itself grant any additional record-level access; the actual records returned still depend on the running user's (or viewer's) own sharing access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Folder sharing is purely about report organization/access to report artifacts; it doesn't expand the underlying record-level sharing model. A is incorrect: Report folders can be shared with public groups, roles, and similar targets. B is incorrect: Folder sharing grants no object permissions at all. C is incorrect: Folder sharing and record-level sharing are distinct, independent mechanisms."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "A legal team needs to control which internal groups can access sensitive contract PDF files stored as Salesforce Files (ContentDocument/ContentVersion) attached to Account records, independent of whether those groups have access to the Account itself. Which mechanism should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use Content Libraries with library-level permissions (Viewer, Author, Administrator) to control file access independently of the related record's own object/record-level sharing."), ("B", "Rely entirely on the Account's OWD, since file visibility is always identical to the visibility of whatever record the file happens to be attached to."), ("C", "Use FLS on the Account object, since file attachments are treated as regular fields subject to field-level security."), ("D", "Files can never be restricted independently of their attached record; there is no separate access model for Salesforce Files.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Content Libraries provide a distinct access model for Salesforce Files with their own membership/permission levels, decoupled from the related Account's sharing. B is incorrect: File access can be decoupled from the related record's sharing when using library-based access. C is incorrect: Files are a distinct object type, not Account fields. D is incorrect: Salesforce Files do have a separate, library-based access model."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "A cross-department project team needs access to a single Salesforce File attached to an Opportunity, but several team members don't have (and shouldn't be given) access to the Opportunity record itself. Which approach grants file access without opening up Opportunity access?", options: [("A", "Grant the team View All on Opportunity, since that is the only way to expose an attached file to users lacking record access."), ("B", "Share the file directly (or place it in a Content Library the team has access to) rather than relying on the record-level attachment link, since file sharing can be managed independently of the record it happens to be attached to."), ("C", "Add the file as a related list on a different, more open object so its visibility inherits from that unrelated object instead."), ("D", "Files inherit access strictly and automatically from every record they're linked to, with no independent sharing option available.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Salesforce Files support independent sharing separate from record-level attachment, so the team can get file access without broader Opportunity access. A is incorrect: Granting View All on Opportunity is a broad, unnecessary escalation. C is incorrect: Relinking to an unrelated object doesn't reflect how file access actually works. D is incorrect: Files are not strictly bound to the sharing of every record they're linked to."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "A field services company is evaluating Territory Management for assigning Accounts to sales territories, and needs to confirm which territory model state permits sharing to actually be generated and enforced. Which state is correct?", options: [("A", "Archived state — sharing continues to be enforced even after a model is archived, to preserve historical access patterns."), ("B", "Planning state — sharing is generated during Planning so it's ready the moment the model goes live."), ("C", "Active state — only an Active territory model generates and enforces territory-based sharing; Planning and Archived states do not drive live record access."), ("D", "All three states (Planning, Active, Archived) generate identical sharing simultaneously, since territory sharing is state-independent.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Only an Active territory model generates and enforces the sharing that grants territory-based record access. A is incorrect: Archived models no longer drive live access. B is incorrect: Planning is for configuring/validating before going live; it doesn't generate enforced sharing. D is incorrect: Sharing behavior is tied specifically to the Active state."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A manufacturer's territory model assigns Accounts to territories using both territory rules and manual assignments. A user is a member of a territory that includes a specific Account, but that user is not the Account's owner and isn't included via the role hierarchy either. What access, if any, does that user have to the Account?", options: [("A", "The user gains access only if OWD for Account is set to Public Read/Write, since territories cannot grant access under a Private OWD."), ("B", "None — territory assignment has no effect on record access; only ownership and role hierarchy grant access to Accounts."), ("C", "The user only gains access if they are also added to a public group referencing the territory, since territory membership alone never grants access."), ("D", "The user gains access to the Account through territory-based sharing itself, since territory membership is an independent access path that operates alongside (and doesn't require) ownership or role hierarchy-based access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Territory Management provides its own distinct access path; territory membership grants access to included Accounts independent of ownership or role hierarchy. A is incorrect: Territory-based sharing functions under Private OWD, which is precisely its value. B is incorrect: Territory-based access is a real, independent sharing mechanism. C is incorrect: Territory membership itself is the access-granting mechanism."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "A company's internal collaboration policy places certain sensitive deal discussions inside a private Chatter group tied to specific Opportunity records. A regional manager has full record-level access to those Opportunities via role hierarchy but is not a member of the private Chatter group. What will the manager experience?", options: [("A", "The manager can still access the Opportunity records themselves via their existing record-level access, but they will not see feed items or posts shared specifically within the private Chatter group, since Chatter group membership independently governs visibility of that group's feed content."), ("B", "The manager automatically sees all private Chatter group content for any record they already have access to, since Chatter respects only record-level sharing and ignores group membership entirely."), ("C", "The manager loses their existing role hierarchy-based access to the Opportunity records because they are not a Chatter group member."), ("D", "Private Chatter groups have no bearing on visibility of any content; all Chatter posts are visible to any user with record access regardless of group privacy settings.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Chatter group privacy operates as its own access layer on top of record-level sharing; a private group's feed items are restricted to actual members, so even a user with full record access won't see group-specific posts unless also a member. B is incorrect: Chatter respects private group membership as an additional restriction. C is incorrect: Chatter group membership has no bearing on record-level sharing access. D is incorrect: Private Chatter groups specifically restrict feed visibility to members."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "A customer-facing Experience Cloud site publishes internal Knowledge articles, and the architect must ensure only articles tagged with a 'Public' data category are visible externally, while internal-only articles remain hidden from portal users. What governs this visibility?", options: [("A", "All Knowledge articles are visible to any authenticated or unauthenticated user by default, and there is no way to restrict visibility by data category."), ("B", "Knowledge article visibility is controlled through a combination of data category group visibility settings, article type permissions, and the profile/permission set assigned to the portal users, all of which must align to expose only the intended 'Public' category articles externally."), ("C", "Article visibility is controlled solely by the article's Owner field, matching it against the portal user's profile name."), ("D", "Knowledge article visibility only applies internally; Experience Cloud sites cannot expose Knowledge content at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "External Knowledge visibility is governed by the interplay of data category group visibility, article type access, and the portal user's profile/permission set. A is incorrect: Knowledge explicitly supports granular visibility restriction via data categories. C is incorrect: Article visibility isn't governed by matching Owner to profile name. D is incorrect: Experience Cloud sites are a fully supported channel for publishing Knowledge externally."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "A sales operations team created a custom list view on Opportunity visible to 'All users,' and a compliance reviewer asks whether this could expose records beyond what OWD allows. What should the architect clarify?", options: [("A", "List views ignore OWD entirely and always show every record in the org to anyone who can see the list view."), ("B", "A list view visible to 'All users' automatically grants those users View All on the underlying object, since list view visibility and object permissions are the same setting."), ("C", "List view visibility settings control who can see and use the list view itself, but the actual records returned in that list view are still filtered by the viewing user's own record-level access (OWD, sharing rules, etc.); the list view being visible to 'All users' does not bypass or expand record-level sharing."), ("D", "List view sharing is only a security concern under Public Read/Write OWD and has no relevance under Private OWD.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Making a list view broadly visible only controls who can find and use it; the underlying query still respects each viewer's actual record-level sharing. A is incorrect: List views are always filtered by the running user's record-level access. B is incorrect: List view visibility and object permissions are distinct settings. D is incorrect: List view sharing is relevant to consider under any OWD level, including Private."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "A compliance officer needs full visibility into every Email Message logged against Contacts across the org, even though Contact has a Private OWD and most users only see emails logged against their own Contacts. Which TWO approaches would independently achieve this?", options: [("A", "Grant the compliance officer View All on EmailMessage directly, since EmailMessage has its own object-level permission that overrides its default sharing behavior."), ("B", "Ensure the compliance officer has appropriate access to the related Contacts (e.g., via View All on Contact), since EmailMessage's sharing generally follows its related parent record."), ("C", "Grant Modify All Data at the system level, since EmailMessage has no independent access control of its own."), ("D", "Modify the Contact's OWD directly, since EmailMessage has no separate permission or sharing behavior distinct from Contact.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "EmailMessage has its own object-level permission behavior (A), and it also generally follows the sharing of its related parent record (B), so either granting View All on EmailMessage directly or ensuring broader Contact access independently achieves full visibility without disrupting the Private baseline for other users. * C: Modify All Data is unnecessarily broad; EmailMessage has its own scoped permission controls. * D: EmailMessage has its own distinct object permission behavior, not solely derived from Contact's OWD."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "A distributor asks whether restricting access to Price Books and Products for certain sales reps (so they only see products relevant to their region) follows the same record-level sharing model as a custom object with Private OWD. What should the architect explain?", options: [("A", "Products and Price Books cannot be restricted by region under any circumstances; every user with Opportunity access automatically sees every product."), ("B", "Price Books and Products always inherit sharing identically to any custom object with no special considerations whatsoever."), ("C", "Price Book and Product visibility is governed exclusively by FLS and has no object-level or sharing-based component at all."), ("D", "Price Books and Products/PricebookEntries have their own object permission and sharing considerations, and while they can be governed by sharing constructs, the approach and defaults differ somewhat from typical custom objects, so the architect must verify object-specific behavior rather than assuming a one-to-one mapping to generic custom object sharing.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Price Books and Products have object-specific nuances alongside standard permission/sharing mechanics, so verification of specific behavior is needed rather than assuming pure equivalence. A is incorrect: Visibility can be scoped and restricted through appropriate configuration. B is incorrect: Meaningful object-specific nuances differentiate these from typical custom objects. C is incorrect: Object-level permissions and sharing do apply; visibility isn't governed solely by FLS."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "An architect is integrating an external ERP system's inventory data into Salesforce using Salesforce Connect and External Objects, and needs to know how access control differs from standard Salesforce objects. What is the key distinction?", options: [("A", "External Objects have a modified sharing model — access is typically governed by object permissions and, depending on configuration, OWD-like settings for external data sources, but full native mechanisms like ownership-based sharing rules or Apex managed sharing don't directly apply the same way they do for standard/custom objects backed by the Salesforce database."), ("B", "External Objects behave identically to standard custom objects in every respect, including full support for ownership-based sharing rules and Apex managed sharing without any limitation."), ("C", "External Objects cannot have any access control applied; every authenticated user automatically sees all rows from the external source."), ("D", "External Objects are secured exclusively through the external system's own login credentials, with Salesforce's permission model playing no role whatsoever.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Object permissions and OWD-like settings still apply, but the fuller native sharing toolkit built around Salesforce record ownership doesn't map directly onto externally sourced rows. B is incorrect: External Objects have documented gaps versus standard objects in sharing capability. C is incorrect: Object permissions and OWD-like settings do apply. D is incorrect: Salesforce's own permission model still governs access to External Objects."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "A retailer's compliance officer must confirm whether a custom Lightning report-scheduling feature can bypass a user's own record-level access when the schedule runs unattended overnight. What should the architect confirm about scheduled reports?", options: [("A", "Scheduled reports inherit the access of whichever user happens to be first to open the resulting emailed report, not the user who created the schedule."), ("B", "Scheduled reports run under the context of the user who scheduled them, so the results reflect that user's own object, field, and record-level access at run time, not an elevated or system-level context."), ("C", "Scheduled reports ignore FLS entirely because they are generated asynchronously outside the normal UI request cycle."), ("D", "Scheduled reports always run in a system-level context regardless of who scheduled them, exposing all records irrespective of the scheduler's sharing access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Report schedules execute using the scheduling user's own security context, filtered by that same user's object permissions, FLS, and sharing access. A is incorrect: The report runs under the scheduler's identity, not the eventual email recipient. C is incorrect: FLS is enforced regardless of synchronous or scheduled execution. D is incorrect: There's no system-level elevation for scheduled reports."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "A construction firm needs to grant its subcontractors—accessing via Experience Cloud—visibility into Salesforce Files attached to Project__c records they're associated with, but subcontractors must never gain broader access to the Project__c object itself beyond specific attached files. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Rely on the Project__c OWD alone, since file visibility is always identical to the visibility of the record it's attached to with no independent control."), ("B", "Grant subcontractors View All on Project__c so they can see the files, since files can only be seen by users with full object-level access to their parent record."), ("C", "Share the specific ContentDocument with the relevant subcontractor users or groups directly, since file-level sharing can be granted independently of the subcontractor's broader Project__c object or record access."), ("D", "Add subcontractors to a Content Library with Author permissions covering all files org-wide, since library-level access is the only way to expose individual files externally.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Salesforce Files support independent sharing separate from the parent record's object permissions. A is incorrect: File visibility can diverge from the parent record's visibility. B is incorrect: Granting View All on Project__c is far broader than needed. D is incorrect: Org-wide Author access exposes every file in that library, not just the intended ones."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "A logistics company's architect must decide between Public Read/Write OWD (to avoid any sharing recalculation overhead) and Private OWD with sharing rules (to enforce tighter access) for a rapidly growing Shipment__c object with millions of records. What is the key scalability trade-off?", options: [("A", "Sharing recalculation only occurs once at object creation time, so ongoing data volume growth has no bearing on this decision."), ("B", "Public Read/Write and Private OWD have identical performance characteristics at any data volume, so the decision should be made purely on functional preference."), ("C", "Private OWD always outperforms Public Read/Write regardless of data volume or sharing rule complexity, since restrictive defaults are inherently more efficient."), ("D", "Public Read/Write avoids ongoing sharing recalculation costs entirely but grants broad access with no enforced boundary, while Private OWD with sharing rules adds recalculation overhead as records and group memberships grow, trading performance cost for enforced least-privilege access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Public Read/Write sidesteps recalculation entirely, while Private OWD's boundaries require continual recomputation as data and membership scale. A is incorrect: Recalculation is triggered by many ongoing events, not a one-time event. B is incorrect: The two approaches have materially different performance profiles at scale. C is incorrect: Private OWD's recalculation can become a real bottleneck at high volume."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "A media company's Data Migration team is loading 5 million legacy Contract__c records overnight and worries that sharing rules will recalculate incrementally during the load, causing some users to briefly see inconsistent access. What feature should the architect use during the load?", options: [("A", "Defer Sharing Calculation, which suspends automatic sharing rule recalculation during the bulk load and lets the team trigger a single recalculation after all records are loaded, avoiding repeated incremental recalculation and inconsistent interim access."), ("B", "Modify All Data granted temporarily to every end user, since that is the standard way to avoid sharing inconsistency during a data load."), ("C", "Disable OWD entirely for the duration of the load, since OWD has no bearing on bulk load performance or sharing recalculation."), ("D", "Convert Contract__c temporarily to a Master-Detail child of a dummy parent object to avoid any sharing recalculation during the load.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Deferring sharing calculation suspends incremental recalculation during the load, allowing one clean recalculation afterward. B is incorrect: Temporarily granting Modify All Data to every user is an unnecessary security exposure. C is incorrect: OWD can't simply be disabled, and it does bear on recalculation. D is incorrect: Recreating relationship type mid-migration is not a supported pattern."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "A fintech company processes millions of Case records per day through Apex triggers that need custom, complex sharing logic based on several related-object field values, logic too intricate for a criteria-based sharing rule to express. The architect is concerned about applying Apex managed sharing synchronously on every insert. What is the correct scalability recommendation?", options: [("A", "Disable sharing calculation for Case entirely and grant every user View All Data instead, since that removes the need for any custom sharing logic."), ("B", "Move the Apex managed sharing logic to an asynchronous process (e.g., Queueable or Batch Apex) triggered after insert, so the sharing computation doesn't block the primary transaction and can be scaled independently of insert throughput."), ("C", "Replace Apex managed sharing entirely with a single very broad criteria-based sharing rule that approximates the complex logic using OR conditions across every relevant field."), ("D", "Keep the sharing logic fully synchronous in the trigger regardless of volume, since Apex managed sharing must always execute in the same transaction as the record insert to be valid.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Decoupling complex, high-volume Apex managed sharing into an asynchronous process avoids blocking the primary insert transaction and lets the sharing computation scale independently. A is incorrect: Granting View All Data to every user eliminates the entire security model. C is incorrect: Criteria-based rules can't express logic dependent on multiple related-object field values. D is incorrect: Apex managed sharing doesn't require synchronous execution in the same transaction."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "An enterprise architect is comparing declarative sharing rules against a custom Apex managed sharing solution for an object expected to reach tens of millions of records with intricate, frequently changing cross-team access patterns. Which factor should most heavily influence the recommendation toward the programmatic approach?", options: [("A", "Criteria-based sharing rules can reference any related object's fields without restriction, so there's no scenario where Apex managed sharing would be architecturally necessary."), ("B", "Declarative sharing rules are always faster at any object size, so programmatic sharing should never be chosen regardless of logic complexity."), ("C", "When access patterns depend on complex combinations of related-object data that criteria-based sharing rules cannot evaluate, and the volume justifies asynchronous processing, Apex managed sharing offers the flexibility and scalability that declarative options can't match."), ("D", "Apex managed sharing is only appropriate for objects with fewer than 10,000 records, per a hard platform-enforced ceiling.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "The deciding factor is logic complexity beyond what criteria-based rules can express, combined with volume that benefits from asynchronous, programmatically controlled sharing. A is incorrect: Criteria-based rules are limited to the record's own fields, not arbitrary related-object logic. B is incorrect: Declarative rules aren't universally faster at high complexity and volume. D is incorrect: There's no such platform-enforced record-count ceiling."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "A staffing agency's High Volume Customer Portal (HVCP) users need to see only their own submitted timesheets, and the architect is asked whether standard role-based sharing or manual sharing can be layered on top for exceptions. Which TWO statements correctly describe HVCP limitations?", options: [("A", "HVCP users don't have roles, so standard role hierarchy-based sharing cannot be used for them."), ("B", "HVCP users automatically receive View All Data on every object they can access, making additional sharing configuration unnecessary."), ("C", "HVCP users can't be granted manual shares, so that mechanism is also unavailable, leaving alternatives like OWD-equivalent settings, account/contact hierarchy-based implicit sharing, or Apex-managed sharing."), ("D", "HVCP is only a licensing distinction with zero impact on the available sharing mechanisms; all standard sharing tools work identically.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "HVCP intentionally omits both per-user roles (A) and manual sharing (B) to stay performant at scale, so architects must lean on alternative mechanisms for these users. B is incorrect: HVCP users don't automatically receive View All Data. D is incorrect: HVCP meaningfully restricts which sharing mechanisms are available."),
        Question(id: "78", question: "A nonprofit onboarding unauthenticated donors through a public-facing Experience Cloud site wants guest users to see only donation campaigns flagged as 'Public' via a picklist field, and asks whether a manual share or a public group membership can help scope this. What should the architect clarify about guest user limitations?", options: [("A", "Guest users automatically see every record on any object exposed to the site, so no sharing configuration is needed to scope visibility to 'Public' campaigns."), ("B", "Guest users can be added to public groups just like any other external user, so a public group with a criteria-based membership rule is the correct approach."), ("C", "Manual sharing works normally for guest users as long as the site administrator performs the share while logged in as an internal admin."), ("D", "Guest users cannot be added to public groups or receive manual shares, so the correct compliant approach is a criteria-based sharing rule (matching the 'Public' picklist value) targeted at the guest user's associated profile-linked access, since that is one of the few sharing mechanisms guest users can use.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Guest users are restricted from group membership and manual sharing, so criteria-based sharing rules are one of the few native mechanisms available to scope their access. A is incorrect: Guest user visibility is governed by OWD and sharing rules like other users. B is incorrect: Guest users cannot be added to public groups at all. C is incorrect: Manual sharing isn't available for guest user access regardless of who performs it."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "A QA engineer must validate that a newly built criteria-based sharing rule on Opportunity (sharing records where 'Deal_Risk__c' equals 'High' with the Risk Management group) actually fires correctly when a Deal_Risk__c value changes during automated testing. What is the correct testing approach?", options: [("A", "Write an Apex test that inserts an Opportunity, updates Deal_Risk__c to 'High' within Test.startTest()/Test.stopTest() to force synchronous sharing recalculation, and then use System.runAs() as a Risk Management group member to assert the record becomes visible via SOQL."), ("B", "Manually eyeball the Opportunity list view as an administrator, since administrators always see exactly what a criteria-based sharing rule would expose to a regular user."), ("C", "Rely solely on the Setup Audit Trail to confirm the sharing rule was activated, since that log conclusively proves the rule is functioning correctly for all future record changes."), ("D", "Skip testing the sharing rule itself, and only test the Deal_Risk__c field's validation rule, since sharing rules cannot be meaningfully unit tested in Apex.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Wrapping the update inside Test.startTest()/stopTest() forces synchronous sharing recalculation, and System.runAs() lets the test assert actual visibility from a real group member's perspective. B is incorrect: Administrators with Modify All Data or View All Data bypass sharing entirely. C is incorrect: The Setup Audit Trail records configuration changes, not functional confirmation of behavior. D is incorrect: Sharing rules can and should be tested via Apex using runAs() and SOQL assertions."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "An architect inherited an org with a complex sharing model spanning role hierarchy, a dozen sharing rules, manual shares, and Apex managed sharing on Account, and a user reports seeing an Account they shouldn't have access to. What is the most efficient diagnostic approach?", options: [("A", "Assume the access is a platform defect and file a support case without first checking any of the org's own sharing configuration."), ("B", "Use the Record Access / 'Why can I see this record?' Sharing Hierarchy tool (or the Sharing Debugger) for that specific user and record, which surfaces every contributing access source (ownership, role hierarchy, sharing rule, manual share, Apex share) in one place."), ("C", "Delete all sharing rules one at a time and observe whether the unexpected access disappears, since there's no diagnostic tool available for this kind of investigation."), ("D", "Manually inspect the user's profile and role assignment only, since object permissions and role position are always the sole explanation for unexpected record access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The Sharing Debugger and Record Access tool are purpose-built to enumerate every sharing source contributing to a specific user's access on a specific record. A is incorrect: Unexpected access is very often explainable by the org's own configuration. C is incorrect: Salesforce provides dedicated diagnostic tooling; deleting production sharing rules experimentally is destructively risky. D is incorrect: Object permissions and role position are only two of several possible contributing factors."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "A payroll SaaS provider grants Partner Community users criteria-based sharing rule access to a custom Timesheet_Approval__c object so partners can see approvals matching their assigned region. A staffing firm using base Customer Community licenses asks for the same setup. What license limitation must the architect flag?", options: [("A", "The limitation only affects Master-Detail relationships, not sharing rules, so criteria-based sharing rules work identically across all portal license types."), ("B", "Customer Community licenses support every sharing mechanism Partner Community supports, so no limitation applies and the setup can proceed unchanged."), ("C", "Base Customer Community licenses do not support criteria-based sharing rules for custom objects the way Partner Community (and Customer Community Plus) licenses do, so the staffing firm's users won't be able to use this sharing mechanism without a license change."), ("D", "Criteria-based sharing rules are unavailable to any external license type regardless of tier, so neither Partner Community nor Customer Community can use them.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Base Customer Community licenses have narrower sharing capabilities than Partner Community or Customer Community Plus. A is incorrect: This distracts with an unrelated relationship-type claim. B is incorrect: License tiers differ meaningfully in supported sharing mechanisms. D is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules are a real, supported mechanism for certain external license types."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "An architect is scoping a Salesforce Platform license rollout for an internal ops team that only needs access to two custom objects, and a stakeholder assumes standard object sharing rules (like ones built for Account) will automatically extend to these users. Which TWO statements correctly describe the Salesforce Platform license's limitation here?", options: [("A", "Salesforce Platform licenses provide access to a more limited set of standard objects than a full Salesforce license, so users may lack access to objects like Account, Contact, or Opportunity entirely."), ("B", "Salesforce Platform and full Salesforce licenses provide identical standard object accessibility, so no gap exists for this rollout."), ("C", "Platform license users can never be valid sharing rule targets for any object, even ones they do have access to."), ("D", "The Platform license's object-accessibility limitation affects which objects a user can access at all, not merely their storage allocation, so a sharing rule referencing an object the user cannot access has no practical effect for that user.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Salesforce Platform licenses provide a more limited set of accessible standard objects (A) than a full Salesforce license, and this distinction meaningfully affects object accessibility itself (D), not merely storage allocation, so a sharing rule referencing an inaccessible object has no effect for these users. B is incorrect: Platform and full Salesforce licenses differ in standard object accessibility. C is incorrect: Platform license users can be valid sharing rule targets for objects they do have access to."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "A large enterprise wants to layer many permission sets and multiple permission set groups onto its Chatter Free external users to fine-tune access, but the architect notices some expected assignments silently fail to apply. What license-related constraint most likely explains this?", options: [("A", "Chatter Free licenses automatically receive every permission set in the org by default, which is why the architect is seeing unexpected behavior."), ("B", "Permission set assignment is entirely license-agnostic, and any license type can be assigned any number of permission sets or permission set groups without restriction."), ("C", "Only profiles matter for restricted license types; permission sets have never had any interaction with license type at all."), ("D", "Certain lower-tier or free license types have restrictions on which permission sets and permission set groups can be assigned, since permission set/permission set group assignment availability is tied to the assigned license type's supported feature set.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "License type governs which permission sets and permission set groups are even assignable to a given user. A is incorrect: There's no default behavior granting every permission set automatically. B is incorrect: License type is not agnostic to permission set assignment. C is incorrect: Permission sets do interact with license type."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "An architect wants to validate a newly built sharing model for Case without modifying any actual sharing configuration. Which TWO native capabilities, used together, provide a safe, non-destructive way to do this?", options: [("A", "'Login As', which lets the architect experience the org exactly as the target user does, subject to admin-specific restrictions."), ("B", "The Record Access page only works for objects with Public Read/Write OWD, making it unreliable for validating a Private OWD sharing model."), ("C", "The Setup Audit Trail, which provides the same per-record access diagnostics as 'Login As' and the Record Access page."), ("D", "The Record Access page, which shows the specific reasons a user has access to a given record.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "'Login As' (A) impersonates the target user's actual session without permanently altering their permissions, and the Record Access page (B) directly surfaces sharing sources for a specific record — together they provide safe, non-destructive validation. B is incorrect: The Record Access page is especially useful under restrictive OWD settings like Private, not unreliable for them. C is incorrect: The Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not per-record access diagnostics — it doesn't provide the same capability."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "A QA team must confirm that a batch of newly inserted Contract__c records correctly inherited Apex managed sharing entries after a nightly job runs, and wants to directly inspect the underlying share table rather than relying on UI-based spot checks alone. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Query the Contract__c object's share table (Contract__Share) directly in a sandbox, examining UserOrGroupId, AccessLevel, and RowCause to enumerate exactly which users/groups have access and why for each record."), ("B", "Rely exclusively on the standard Case object's share table, since all custom object sharing entries are actually stored there regardless of object."), ("C", "Query the FeedItem object, since Chatter feed entries are the authoritative source of truth for all sharing grants on any object."), ("D", "There's no way to programmatically inspect sharing sources for a custom object; only the UI-based Sharing Debugger can be used.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Every object with sharing enabled has a corresponding Share object that can be queried directly via SOQL, giving a complete, auditable picture of who has access and through which sharing source. B is incorrect: Each object has its own dedicated Share object. C is incorrect: FeedItem tracks Chatter posts, not sharing grants. D is incorrect: Share objects are fully queryable via SOQL."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "An architect writing Apex tests for a trigger that inserts Apex managed shares on Opportunity wants to confirm the test accurately reflects real sharing behavior rather than producing a false positive due to test isolation quirks. What should the architect keep in mind about System.runAs() and sharing in tests?", options: [("A", "System.runAs() has no effect on sharing evaluation whatsoever; it only changes limits.setLimit() values, so all test data remains completely admin-visible under any assertion."), ("B", "System.runAs() changes the running user context for permission and sharing evaluation within the test, but a 'with sharing' class's sharing enforcement still depends on its own declaration, and test data created in @testSetup runs as the test's original context, so architects must structure test data setup and assertions carefully to avoid a false sense of correctness."), ("C", "All Apex tests always run with sharing fully bypassed no matter what, so runAs() is irrelevant to sharing validation."), ("D", "Data created inside @testSetup automatically becomes visible only to whichever user is used in the very first System.runAs() call within the test class, permanently.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "runAs() changes the effective user for checks, but the class's own sharing declaration still governs enforcement, and test data setup timing matters. A is incorrect: runAs() explicitly affects permission/sharing checks. C is incorrect: A 'with sharing' class still enforces sharing during a test. D is incorrect: Test data visibility isn't permanently locked to the first runAs() call."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "A retailer's compliance team wants to periodically audit whether users accessed Contact records they shouldn't have been able to see, going beyond simple report exports to include general record views via the UI. What should the architect explain about the available auditing capability?", options: [("A", "Field History Tracking natively logs every time a user views a Contact record, satisfying the audit requirement directly."), ("B", "Event Monitoring captures every single record view across the entire UI by default with no additional configuration needed, fully satisfying the compliance requirement out of the box."), ("C", "Real-Time Event Monitoring can capture detailed events like Login history and Report Export, but comprehensive per-record view auditing for ordinary UI page views has more limited native coverage, so the architect should clarify exactly which event types are captured natively versus what would require supplemental tracking."), ("D", "There is no native Salesforce capability that offers any auditing of user activity; compliance must build a fully custom logging solution from scratch.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Event Monitoring has strong coverage for specific event types but narrower native coverage for general UI record views, so expectations need to be set accordingly. A is incorrect: Field History Tracking logs value changes, not views. B is incorrect: Event Monitoring doesn't capture every UI record view by default. D is incorrect: Salesforce does provide native auditing capabilities with specific scopes."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "After a large-scale sharing model migration on Opportunity involving new sharing rules, updated OWD, and Apex managed sharing, an architect must choose the most comprehensive verification approach before go-live. Which TWO practices, combined, provide the most comprehensive coverage?", options: [("A", "A single administrator's manual review of the Opportunity list view, since an admin's view always reflects exactly what regular users would see post-migration."), ("B", "The Record Access page for spot-checking specific user/record pairs."), ("C", "Direct SOQL queries against OpportunityShare to audit sharing sources at scale."), ("D", "The Setup Audit Trail alone, since it provides complete confirmation that the new sharing model behaves correctly for every user.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Targeted per-record/per-user checks via the Record Access page (A) combined with bulk auditability through direct share-table queries (B) together cover both the specificity and breadth needed to catch misconfigurations before go-live. A is incorrect: An admin with elevated permissions bypasses sharing entirely, so their list view can't validate what a regular user would actually see. D is incorrect: The Setup Audit Trail only records that changes were made, not functional correctness."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "A media company's Content Library holds thousands of assets, and the compliance team wants to grant a specific vendor team the ability to add new files to a designated library folder and edit their own uploads, but not delete other people's files or manage library membership. Which library permission level fits?", options: [("A", "There is no intermediate level; library members must be either full Administrators or read-only Viewers."), ("B", "Viewer, since Viewer permission includes upload rights as long as the file is later approved by an Administrator."), ("C", "Administrator, since it's the only library permission level that allows uploading files at all."), ("D", "Author, which allows adding and managing content within the library at a working-contributor level without granting the full administrative control that Administrator provides.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Author is designed for contributors needing to add/manage content without broader administrative capabilities. A is incorrect: Content Libraries support intermediate levels like Author. B is incorrect: Viewer is read-only with no upload capability. C is incorrect: Administrator isn't required for uploading."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "An HR department wants Reports and Dashboards stored in a specific folder to be visible only to HR Business Partners, while HR leadership needs the ability to edit and reorganize the folder's contents. Which folder sharing configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "Share the folder with the HR Business Partners group at Viewer access and with the HR leadership group at Manager access, since folder-level access levels independently control what each group can do with the folder's contents."), ("B", "Grant everyone the same Viewer access, since folder sharing only supports a single uniform access level across all shared parties."), ("C", "Rely solely on the underlying Opportunity or Case object's OWD, since folder access is always identical to whatever the underlying object's OWD dictates."), ("D", "Folders cannot differentiate access levels between groups; only individual users can have distinct access levels on a shared folder.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Folder sharing supports distinct access levels (Viewer, Editor, Manager) per shared group, matching the differing needs directly. B is incorrect: Folder sharing supports multiple access levels for different groups simultaneously. C is incorrect: Folder access and underlying record OWD are independent concerns. D is incorrect: Folders can be shared with groups, each at different access levels."),
        Question(id: "91", question: "A regulated brokerage firm needs Big Objects storing years of historical trade data to be accessible only to a designated 'Trade Compliance' permission set, with standard traders unable to query the data at all, even though standard trade records reference the Big Object via a custom relationship field. Which TWO statements correctly describe how to secure Big Objects?", options: [("A", "Big Objects automatically inherit the OWD and full sharing rule capabilities of any standard object they're related to."), ("B", "Big Objects support standard object permissions (Read/Create) that can be granted via permission sets."), ("C", "Big Objects have no permission model at all and are always readable by any authenticated user regardless of profile or permission set."), ("D", "Big Objects don't support the full record-level sharing model of standard/custom objects, so object-level permission is the primary access control.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Big Objects support object-level Read/Create permissions via permission sets (A), and because they lack the full record-level sharing toolkit (B), scoping Read to the compliance permission set is the correct and sufficient control. A is incorrect: Big Objects don't automatically inherit sharing behavior from a related standard object. C is incorrect: Big Objects do have an object-level permission model."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "An architect designing for Large Data Volumes (LDVs) on a custom object with millions of records and a deep role hierarchy is warned that sharing recalculation could become a major performance bottleneck during a data migration. Which LDV-specific design consideration should the architect prioritize before load, distinct from ongoing OWD/sharing rule design?", options: [("A", "Increasing the role hierarchy depth further before the load, since deeper hierarchies always process bulk loads faster."), ("B", "Using the 'Defer Sharing Calculation' capability during the bulk load so sharing recalculation is deferred until after the load completes, then triggering recalculation once, rather than recalculating incrementally on every batch of inserted records."), ("C", "Disabling all object permissions during the load, since permissions (not sharing) are what drive recalculation overhead."), ("D", "Setting OWD to Private only after the load completes, since Private OWD during a load has no bearing on sharing recalculation volume.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Deferring sharing calculation during a large bulk load avoids incremental recalculation per batch, a well-known LDV pattern. A is incorrect: Deeper hierarchies increase recalculation overhead. C is incorrect: Object permissions and sharing recalculation are different mechanisms. D is incorrect: OWD level directly affects recalculation volume during a load."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "A logistics company ingests millions of GPS tracking Big Object records daily and needs read access scoped so that only a 'Fleet Analytics' permission set can query the data through SOQL and reports, while every other profile in the org has zero visibility. After configuring object-level Read only for that permission set, what should the architect verify to confirm the design is complete?", options: [("A", "That a role hierarchy has been built specifically for the Big Object, since Big Objects require hierarchy-based access in addition to permission grants."), ("B", "That a Private OWD has also been applied to the Big Object, since Big Objects require an explicit OWD setting in addition to object permissions."), ("C", "That no other profile or permission set in the org has been separately granted Read on the Big Object, since Big Object access is governed purely by that object-level permission with no additional record-level sharing layer to double-check."), ("D", "That sharing rules have been created granting Read to the Fleet Analytics permission set, since object permissions alone are insufficient for Big Objects.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Since Big Object access is governed solely by object-level Read/Create permissions, the complete check is confirming no other profile/permission set was inadvertently granted that same Read. A is incorrect: Big Objects don't use role hierarchy-based access. B is incorrect: Big Objects don't have a configurable OWD setting. D is incorrect: Sharing rules don't apply to Big Objects."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "A retail bank wants sensitive PII fields masked in non-production sandboxes used by an offshore QA team, while keeping full production data intact and accessible to authorized production users. Which capability should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Classic Encryption applied to the sandbox only, since Classic Encryption is specifically designed for masking data during sandbox refresh cycles."), ("B", "Shield Platform Encryption applied directly to production, since encrypting production data also automatically masks the same fields in every sandbox refresh."), ("C", "FLS configured only in the sandbox's QA profile, since FLS applied in a sandbox automatically masks the underlying data values for any future refresh."), ("D", "Salesforce's Data Mask (sandbox data masking) capability, which anonymizes or obfuscates sensitive field values specifically within copied sandbox environments while leaving production data untouched.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Data Mask is purpose-built to anonymize sensitive field values specifically in sandbox copies, leaving production fully intact. A is incorrect: Classic Encryption is a display-masking feature, not a sandbox-refresh-wide anonymization tool. B is incorrect: Encryption on production doesn't automatically mask sandbox data on refresh. C is incorrect: FLS doesn't alter or obfuscate underlying data values."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "A nonprofit wants donors interacting through a public donation portal (Guest User access) to be able to view a public 'Campaign Progress' page showing aggregate totals without exposing any individual Donation__c record details. What's the safest architectural pattern?", options: [("A", "Expose only aggregate, pre-computed totals (e.g., via a rollup field on Campaign__c or a summary object) to the guest user profile, rather than granting the guest user any direct Read access to individual Donation__c records, since guest user access should be minimized to exactly the data intended for public exposure."), ("B", "Grant the guest user profile Read access to all fields on Donation__c, since guest users can only see aggregated totals regardless of the underlying object permissions granted."), ("C", "Grant the guest user profile View All Data so the rollup calculates correctly in real time."), ("D", "Guest users cannot see any data at all under any configuration, so this requirement is architecturally impossible.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Minimizing guest user exposure to only pre-aggregated data avoids ever granting direct record access, the safest pattern for an unauthenticated context. B is incorrect: Granting Read on all fields would expose individual records through reports/APIs. C is incorrect: View All Data is disproportionate and unnecessary for a rollup to display. D is incorrect: Guest users can be granted carefully scoped access safely."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "A conglomerate wants a centralized audit capability to answer 'which users viewed which List Views today across the org' as part of a data-loss-prevention initiative. Which native capability should the architect point them to?", options: [("A", "The Setup Audit Trail, since it tracks every individual list view access by any user across the org in real time."), ("B", "Real-Time Event Monitoring / Event Log Files (e.g., ListViewEvent or related access events), which capture granular access events including list view interactions for audit and DLP analysis."), ("C", "Field History Tracking, since it captures every list view a user has opened for any tracked field."), ("D", "There is no native way to audit list view access; this would require a fully custom-built logging solution from scratch.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Event Monitoring / Event Log Files capture granular access events including list view activity, supporting DLP analysis without a fully custom solution. A is incorrect: Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not routine end-user activity. C is incorrect: Field History Tracking has no relationship to list view access. D is incorrect: Native Event Monitoring already provides this visibility."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "A wealth management firm's Financial Account custom object stores 15 years of client transaction history and is nearing the practical limits of standard object storage performance for reporting. The architect proposes moving historical (7+ year old) transaction records to a Big Object while keeping recent transactions in the standard custom object. What security model implication must the architect flag to stakeholders?", options: [("A", "Moving data to a Big Object has no security implications whatsoever since Big Objects are purely a storage optimization with an identical permission model to custom objects."), ("B", "Big Objects automatically replicate every sharing rule from the original custom object with no configuration changes needed."), ("C", "Once historical data moves to a Big Object, the rich record-level sharing model (OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual shares) that governed the standard object no longer applies to that historical data; access will instead be controlled solely by object-level Read/Create permissions, so any nuanced, per-client access pattern currently relying on sharing rules must be redesigned around coarser permission-based access for the archived data."), ("D", "Big Objects require OWD to be set to Public Read/Write before any data can be migrated into them.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Migrating to a Big Object trades nuanced sharing-rule-driven access for a coarser object-level permission model, a real trade-off stakeholders must understand. A is incorrect: This contradicts the real difference in permission models. B is incorrect: Big Objects have no mechanism to inherit sharing rules. D is incorrect: Big Objects don't have an OWD setting at all."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "A city government's public records request portal must expose specific approved Case records to any member of the public via Guest User access, while ensuring the underlying Attachments/Files on those Cases (some containing internal notes) remain hidden unless explicitly marked public. Which TWO configuration steps, together, achieve this?", options: [("A", "Since guest users can see the Case, they automatically see every File and Attachment linked to that Case with no additional configuration required."), ("B", "Setting external OWD for Case to Public Read/Write is the only step needed, since File visibility is governed entirely by the parent object's external OWD setting."), ("C", "Use a criteria-based sharing rule scoped to a 'Publicly_Released__c' field on Case for the guest user context."), ("D", "Separately ensure Files/Attachments are only exposed via a distinct mechanism (e.g., a related list or Public flag check) rather than assuming Case-level guest access automatically exposes every attached file.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Case-level and File-level visibility are distinct access layers, so the criteria-based rule (A) scopes Case visibility while Files must be separately scoped (B) rather than assuming automatic cascade from Case access. A is incorrect: File visibility isn't automatically implied by Case-level access. B is incorrect: External OWD on Case governs Case visibility, not File visibility."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "An architect needs to confirm whether List Views built for a 'Regional Ops' team, filtered to show only records where Region__c equals a specific value, could be used by a malicious insider to infer data about records the underlying OWD should otherwise hide from them. What is the correct technical explanation?", options: [("A", "A malicious insider could use list view filters to enumerate hidden records' existence even without having record-level access, since filter criteria always evaluate against the full dataset before applying sharing."), ("B", "List view filters execute with system-level access regardless of the viewer's actual permissions, so any user could see restricted data by guessing the right filter criteria."), ("C", "List views bypass sharing entirely for any filter criteria referencing a picklist field specifically."), ("D", "List views execute their query under the viewing user's own record-level access, so no matter how a list view is filtered, it can only ever return records the viewing user already has legitimate access to — filtering criteria cannot bypass OWD or sharing to leak restricted records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "List view queries are scoped by the viewing user's sharing access first; filters only narrow that already-permitted set. A is incorrect: Sharing is applied before filter criteria narrow the result set. B is incorrect: List views always respect the viewing user's own sharing. C is incorrect: There's no special exemption for picklist-based filters."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "A university's Alumni Relations team wants to expose select Knowledge articles about upcoming events to alumni logged into a portal, while keeping internal-process articles (like staff onboarding guides) completely invisible to alumni regardless of data category. What's the correct layered approach?", options: [("A", "Assign the alumni portal profile a data category group visibility scoped only to the 'Alumni Events' category (excluding internal categories), and ensure the article type permissions granted to that profile don't include internal-only article types, so both category-level and article-type-level restrictions work together to exclude internal-process content."), ("B", "Data category visibility alone is sufficient; article type permissions have no bearing on which articles a portal user can see."), ("C", "All Knowledge articles are visible to any authenticated portal user by default, regardless of data category or article type configuration."), ("D", "Knowledge article access can only be restricted by removing internal articles from the Knowledge base entirely, since no profile-based restriction mechanism exists.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Knowledge visibility depends on both data category group visibility and article type permissions working together. B is incorrect: Article type permissions do meaningfully restrict visibility independent of data category. C is incorrect: Knowledge articles aren't universally visible by default. D is incorrect: Profile/permission-set-based restriction via categories and article types is a real, supported mechanism."),
        Question(id: "101", question: "A regional bank's Loan_Application__c object has a Private OWD, and a newly formed 'Underwriting Committee' spans members from three different roles that don't share a common branch in the hierarchy. The committee needs Read/Write access to applications flagged 'Under Committee Review' only. Which combination is correct?", options: [("A", "Add all three roles to the role hierarchy under a single new parent role, since role hierarchy is the only way to share records with users spanning multiple roles."), ("B", "Create a public group containing the specific committee members (regardless of role), and build a criteria-based sharing rule sharing Loan_Application__c records where the review flag is set, targeting that public group at Read/Write."), ("C", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write for Loan_Application__c, since criteria-based sharing rules cannot target arbitrary combinations of users across roles."), ("D", "Use Account Teams, since Account Teams are the correct mechanism for any object requiring cross-role visibility.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A public group aggregates arbitrary members across roles, and a criteria-based sharing rule keyed to the review flag grants exactly the conditional access the committee needs without restructuring the hierarchy. A is incorrect: Restructuring the role hierarchy just for one committee disrupts existing reporting-based access elsewhere. C is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules can target public groups regardless of role composition; this claim is false. D is incorrect: Account Teams are scoped to Account/Opportunity, not arbitrary custom objects like Loan_Application__c."),
        Question(id: "102", question: "A national retailer's Store_Incident__c object needs regional loss-prevention managers to see incidents only from stores in their assigned region, using a 'Region__c' lookup field, while corporate LP directors need to see every incident nationwide. What is the most maintainable sharing design?", options: [("A", "Manually share every incident record individually to each regional manager as it's created, since criteria-based sharing rules cannot filter on lookup fields."), ("B", "Set OWD to Public Read Only for everyone, since loss-prevention data should always default to fully open visibility for operational transparency."), ("C", "Set OWD to Private, use criteria-based sharing rules (or role hierarchy positioning) keyed on Region__c to grant regional managers access to their region's incidents, and grant corporate directors View All on Store_Incident__c for nationwide visibility."), ("D", "Grant every LP manager Modify All Data, since that is the only way to guarantee any region-based access pattern works correctly.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Private OWD with region-based criteria sharing scopes regional managers correctly, while View All cleanly gives corporate directors the broader nationwide visibility without disturbing the regional restriction. A is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules can filter on lookup field values; manual per-record sharing doesn't scale. B is incorrect: Public Read Only exposes every incident to everyone, defeating the region-based restriction entirely. D is incorrect: Modify All Data is a drastic overcorrection carrying unnecessary edit/delete risk."),
        Question(id: "103", question: "An architect designing sharing for a nonprofit's Volunteer_Hours__c object must ensure that a volunteer coordinator automatically loses access to a volunteer's logged hours the moment that volunteer is reassigned to a different coordinator, with no lingering access. Which mechanism guarantees this cleanly?", options: [("A", "A criteria-based sharing rule matching the old coordinator's name, which will stop matching once the name is manually removed from the rule."), ("B", "Manual sharing performed once when the volunteer is first assigned, since manual shares automatically expire the instant a related lookup field changes."), ("C", "Apex managed sharing with no defined row cause, since shares without a row cause are automatically removed whenever any field on the record changes."), ("D", "Ownership-based sharing rules tied to role hierarchy position, since access recalculates automatically whenever the record's owner (or the owner's role) changes, removing the prior coordinator's inherited access without manual intervention.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Role hierarchy-based (or ownership-based sharing rule) access is dynamically recalculated whenever ownership or role assignment changes, automatically removing the prior coordinator's access with no manual cleanup step needed. A is incorrect: A criteria-based rule matching a specific name would need ongoing manual maintenance, not automatic removal. B is incorrect: Manual shares don't automatically expire based on unrelated field changes. C is incorrect: Apex shares without a row cause are actually tied to the record owner and get deleted on ownership change, but this isn't a general \"any field change\" behavior, misdescribing the actual mechanism."),
        Question(id: "104", question: "A software company's Bug__c object has OWD set to Private. Engineering leads want any bug marked 'Severity = Critical' to become visible to the entire QA organization instantly, without needing QA to be added to any group beforehand. Which approach best satisfies the 'instantly, no pre-added group' requirement?", options: [("A", "A criteria-based sharing rule matching Severity = Critical, sharing with a broad 'All QA' public group that already contains the entire QA org as members, so any bug meeting the criteria becomes visible as soon as the recalculation completes with no per-bug configuration."), ("B", "Manual sharing performed by the engineering lead on each Critical bug individually, since that is the fastest way to grant instant access without any group configuration."), ("C", "Role hierarchy alone, since QA automatically sees every Critical bug the moment severity changes without any additional sharing configuration."), ("D", "Apex managed sharing triggered only once per fiscal quarter via a scheduled batch job, since real-time responsiveness isn't achievable through any native mechanism.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A criteria-based sharing rule targeting a group that already includes the whole QA org satisfies the \"no group changes needed\" condition, automatically granting access whenever a bug matches the Critical criteria. B is incorrect: Manual sharing per bug is not automatic and doesn't scale. C is incorrect: Role hierarchy alone doesn't grant automatic criteria-based visibility without a corresponding sharing rule. D is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules recalculate promptly on relevant changes; a quarterly batch job is unnecessarily slow and not how native sharing rules work."),
        Question(id: "105", question: "A logistics company uses Master-Detail from Delivery_Stop__c to Route__c, and Route__c uses a Lookup to Driver_Profile__c. A dispatcher has access to a Driver_Profile__c record via a sharing rule but has never been granted access to the associated Route__c records. What access does the dispatcher have to the Delivery_Stop__c records under that driver's routes?", options: [("A", "Full access, since Master-Detail cascading always reaches back through any number of Lookup relationships further up the chain automatically."), ("B", "None automatically — since Driver_Profile__c to Route__c is a Lookup (not Master-Detail), access to the Driver_Profile__c doesn't cascade to Route__c, so there is no accessible Route__c parent for the Delivery_Stop__c Master-Detail cascade to build on."), ("C", "Read-only access, since Lookup relationships to a driver always grant at least Read on every Master-Detail descendant three levels down."), ("D", "Full access, since sharing rules on Driver_Profile__c automatically extend to every object anywhere in the org that references it via any relationship type.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Master-Detail cascading requires an accessible parent to cascade from; since the Route__c-to-Driver_Profile__c link is a Lookup, Driver_Profile__c access doesn't extend to Route__c, breaking the chain before it can reach Delivery_Stop__c. A is incorrect: Cascading doesn't traverse backward through Lookup relationships. C is incorrect: There's no such automatic three-levels-deep Lookup-based grant. D is incorrect: Sharing rules don't automatically extend across unrelated relationship chains this way."),
        Question(id: "106", question: "An HR platform's Performance_Review__c object needs a design where an employee's direct manager can edit the review, the employee's skip-level manager can read but not edit, and HR Business Partners can read/edit any review company-wide. Which combination is correct?", options: [("A", "Grant every manager Modify All Data, since that is the only way to differentiate between direct and skip-level manager access levels."), ("B", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write for everyone, since performance data should default to fully open access to simplify the hierarchy design."), ("C", "Set OWD to Private, rely on role hierarchy (with Grant Access Using Hierarchies enabled) so the direct manager (as owner or via ownership-based sharing) has Edit and the skip-level manager inherits Read through the hierarchy, and grant HR Business Partners View All plus Edit via a permission set for company-wide read/edit."), ("D", "Use Account Teams to represent the manager relationships, since Account Teams natively support multi-level management hierarchy access differentiation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Role hierarchy naturally provides read access one or more levels up while ownership determines edit rights at the direct level, and View All plus Edit via permission set gives HR Business Partners the broader company-wide read/edit without disturbing the hierarchy-based structure. A is incorrect: Modify All Data doesn't differentiate access levels between direct and skip-level managers at all. B is incorrect: Public Read/Write exposes every review to everyone, which is inappropriate for sensitive performance data. D is incorrect: Account Teams are scoped to Account/Opportunity relationships, not employee management hierarchies."),
        Question(id: "107", question: "A SaaS company's Renewal_Opportunity__c object uses criteria-based sharing rules keyed on 'Renewal_Risk__c' to grant the Customer Success leadership team visibility into at-risk renewals. After a bulk data load changes Renewal_Risk__c on 50,000 records simultaneously, leadership reports intermittent, inconsistent visibility for about twenty minutes. What should the architect tell them?", options: [("A", "The inconsistency proves criteria-based sharing rules cannot be used with bulk data loads under any circumstances and must be replaced with manual sharing."), ("B", "This indicates permanent data corruption in the sharing table that requires a support case and a full sharing recalculation from Setup regardless of the underlying cause."), ("C", "Criteria-based sharing rules only apply to records created after the rule was activated, so the inconsistency is permanent for all 50,000 records."), ("D", "This is expected: criteria-based sharing rule recalculation for a large batch of simultaneous field changes happens asynchronously and can take time to fully propagate, so temporary inconsistency during that window is normal platform behavior, not a data integrity defect.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Sharing recalculation for large batches of changes is asynchronous and can take time to fully process; a temporary propagation window is expected, not a sign of corruption. A is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules are compatible with bulk loads; the temporary lag is expected, not disqualifying. B is incorrect: This isn't data corruption; it typically resolves once recalculation completes, without necessarily requiring a support case. C is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules re-evaluate on relevant changes to existing records, not just new ones."),
        Question(id: "108", question: "A consulting firm's Engagement__c object is shared with a public group called 'All Consultants' via a sharing rule granting Read Only, but a client complaint prompts a request to also let a specific 'Delivery Leads' subset edit engagements for accounts they're not the owner of, without changing the broader read-only grant. What is the correct additive approach?", options: [("A", "Edit the existing 'All Consultants' sharing rule to change its access level to Read/Write, since a single sharing rule can only ever apply one access level to every group it targets, including the general consultant population."), ("B", "Create a second, separate sharing rule (or use a distinct public group for Delivery Leads) granting Read/Write specifically to the Delivery Leads group, layering on top of the existing Read Only rule for All Consultants, since multiple sharing rules can coexist and the most permissive access level any rule grants for a given user wins."), ("C", "Remove Delivery Leads from the All Consultants group entirely, since a single sharing rule cannot support layered access levels across different subsets of the same broader population."), ("D", "Grant Delivery Leads Modify All Data, since sharing rules cannot express different access levels for different subsets of a shared population.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Multiple sharing rules can coexist on the same object; a distinct rule targeting only Delivery Leads at a higher access level layers on top of the broader Read Only rule, with the most permissive applicable access winning for any given user. A is incorrect: Changing the existing rule's access level would broaden edit access to the entire consultant population, not just Delivery Leads. C is incorrect: Removing Delivery Leads from the broader group is unnecessary; a second targeted rule solves this cleanly. D is incorrect: Modify All Data is a drastic overcorrection; layered sharing rules already support this pattern."),
        Question(id: "109", question: "An insurance carrier's Policy__c object OWD is Private, and the architect needs claims adjusters to see only policies linked to claims they're actively working, dynamically, as claim assignments change daily. Which mechanism is best suited to this frequently-changing, relationship-driven access pattern?", options: [("A", "Role hierarchy alone, since claims adjusters' dynamic policy assignments are always fully captured by their static position in the hierarchy."), ("B", "A single static criteria-based sharing rule set once and never updated, since criteria-based sharing rules automatically track claim assignment changes on a fully unrelated object with zero additional configuration."), ("C", "Apex managed sharing triggered by claim assignment changes, dynamically inserting and removing PolicyShare records as an adjuster's active claim assignments change, since this pattern requires programmatic control tied to a related object's changing state that criteria-based sharing rules can't express."), ("D", "Public Read/Write OWD, since it is the only way to guarantee adjusters always see policies tied to their current claims regardless of reassignment frequency.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Because visibility must track a related object's frequently changing state (claim assignment), Apex managed sharing driven by that assignment logic is the correct mechanism — criteria-based sharing rules can't evaluate arbitrary related-object relationships this way. A is incorrect: Static hierarchy position doesn't reflect daily-changing claim assignments. B is incorrect: Criteria-based sharing rules evaluate the shared record's own fields, not a related object's changing assignment state. D is incorrect: Public Read/Write exposes every policy to every adjuster, far broader than the dynamic, claim-specific requirement."),
        Question(id: "110", question: "A logistics company built an intricate sharing model on Shipment__c combining role hierarchy, twelve sharing rules, and Apex managed sharing, and a new compliance requirement demands that no single mechanism be allowed to grant access to shipments flagged 'Legal Hold' regardless of ownership or role. What is the architecturally sound way to enforce this hold?", options: [("A", "Set OWD to Private only for Legal Hold shipments, since OWD can be configured differently on a per-record basis."), ("B", "Add a criteria-based sharing rule with 'Legal Hold' criteria that grants no access, since sharing rules can both grant and revoke access depending on how the criteria is written."), ("C", "Remove all twelve existing sharing rules whenever any shipment is placed on Legal Hold, since sharing rules apply per object, not per record."), ("D", "Since sharing mechanisms are additive and cannot restrict access below OWD or other grants, enforce the Legal Hold restriction using a validation rule or Flow blocking edits, combined with FLS or a separate highly restricted object/field pattern for hold-flagged data, since sharing rules alone cannot carve out an exception that overrides other grants.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Because sharing mechanisms can only add access, never subtract it, truly restricting a subset of records regardless of other grants requires a different control point (validation/Flow blocking edits, or a distinct restricted data pattern), not more sharing configuration. A is incorrect: OWD is set once per object, not configurable per individual record. B is incorrect: Sharing rules are exclusively additive; they cannot be configured to revoke access that other mechanisms already granted. C is incorrect: Sharing rules apply object-wide, not per-record, so this action wouldn't isolate Legal Hold records specifically and would disrupt normal operations."),
        Question(id: "111", question: "A marketing agency wants a specific 'Analytics' permission set group to run reports pulling data from Opportunity, Campaign, and a custom Ad_Spend__c object, but the agency's compliance policy says report access should never implicitly grant broader object access beyond what's needed for reporting. What should the architect verify about report-level access?", options: [("A", "That granting access to the report folder automatically also grants View All Data on every object referenced by any report in that folder."), ("B", "That report execution still respects the running user's own object permissions and record-level sharing on every underlying object (Opportunity, Campaign, Ad_Spend__c), so granting report folder access alone doesn't bypass or expand the actual data access needed on each object."), ("C", "That reports bypass object permissions entirely once a user is added to the folder, since folder access is the sole determinant of report data visibility."), ("D", "That reports require Modify All Data on every referenced object before they can even be run, regardless of the report's actual field selection.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Report execution always respects the running user's actual object permissions and sharing on each underlying object; folder access controls report visibility/management, not the underlying data access itself. A is incorrect: Folder access grants no object permissions at all. C is incorrect: Object permissions and sharing are always enforced during report execution regardless of folder membership. D is incorrect: Reports are a read operation; Modify All Data has no bearing on running a report."),
        Question(id: "112", question: "A city's public works department publishes select Knowledge articles about road closures to an unauthenticated Experience Cloud site using Guest User access, while keeping internal maintenance procedure articles hidden. After configuring data category visibility for the guest profile, the architect discovers guest users can still see internal articles through global search. What additional layer must be checked?", options: [("A", "Guest users cannot be restricted from global search results under any circumstances, making this an unavoidable platform limitation."), ("B", "Nothing further is needed; global search always respects data category visibility alone with no separate article type consideration."), ("C", "Article type-level access for the guest profile, since data category visibility and article type permissions both gate Knowledge visibility independently — search results respect both, so an internal article type still accessible to the guest profile would surface even with restrictive category visibility."), ("D", "The issue must be a caching bug, since data category and article type settings always take effect immediately with no possibility of a residual access gap.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Knowledge visibility is gated by both data category group visibility and article type permissions independently; leaving an internal article type accessible to the guest profile lets those articles surface via search even if categories are scoped correctly. A is incorrect: Guest user search visibility can be scoped correctly once both layers are configured. B is incorrect: Article type permissions are a separate, independently enforced layer. D is incorrect: This is a real configuration gap, not a caching artifact."),
        Question(id: "113", question: "A national retailer's Territory Management model assigns Accounts to territories, and a sales rep who is a territory member but not the Account owner asks why they can edit the Account but a colleague in the same territory can only view it. What's the most likely explanation?", options: [("A", "Only the Account owner and System Administrators can ever have Edit access under Territory Management, regardless of territory configuration."), ("B", "Territory Management always grants identical access to every member of a given territory with no way to differentiate access levels."), ("C", "The discrepancy must be caused by a sharing rule entirely unrelated to Territory Management, since territories themselves never differentiate access levels."), ("D", "Territory-based access can specify differentiated access levels (e.g., Read vs. Read/Write) per territory or per user's role within the territory model, so two members of the same territory can legitimately have different access levels to the same Account.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Territory Management supports configuring differentiated access levels within a territory, so members can legitimately have different capabilities on the same Account based on their configured role/access level in that territory. A is incorrect: Territory-granted Edit access is not restricted solely to the owner or admins. B is incorrect: Territory access levels can be differentiated per member/role, not uniformly identical. C is incorrect: This is a plausible, directly explainable outcome of territory configuration, not necessarily an unrelated rule."),
        Question(id: "114", question: "A biotech company wants its Tableau-connected reporting layer to reflect exactly the same record-level restrictions that apply inside Salesforce for a Private OWD custom object, so that a scientist who can't see certain Study__c records in Salesforce also can't see them through the connected analytics tool. What must the architect confirm about the integration?", options: [("A", "That Tableau automatically inherits Salesforce's sharing model for any connection type with zero configuration, since all Salesforce connectors respect sharing identically by default regardless of how the service account is set up."), ("B", "That the connected analytics integration is configured to run under each individual user's own Salesforce credentials/permissions (rather than a single shared service account with broad access), since only per-user-context queries will naturally inherit the same record-level sharing restrictions as native Salesforce access."), ("C", "That FLS alone is sufficient to replicate record-level restrictions in any external analytics tool, since FLS and OWD are functionally identical for this purpose."), ("D", "That the analytics tool's own login credentials, independent of Salesforce, are what determine which Study__c records are visible.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Whether an external tool respects Salesforce's record-level sharing depends entirely on whether it queries using per-user context; a shared service account with broad access would bypass the individual-level restrictions entirely. A is incorrect: This isn't automatic across every connection type; it depends specifically on how the integration authenticates and queries. C is incorrect: FLS and OWD serve different purposes (field-level vs. record-level) and aren't interchangeable substitutes for each other in this context. D is incorrect: The external tool's own credentials don't govern Salesforce record visibility; the Salesforce-side query context does."),
        Question(id: "115", question: "A manufacturing company's Asset__c records (standard object) need to be restricted so that field service technicians only see Assets at customer sites they're assigned to, while keeping the existing Account-level sharing untouched. The architect confirms Asset has its own OWD setting separate from Account. What should guide the design?", options: [("A", "Set Account's OWD to Private and assume Asset automatically becomes equally restrictive with zero additional configuration."), ("B", "Assume Asset access always exactly mirrors Account access with no independent configuration possible, since Asset is a child of Account by default in every org."), ("C", "Configure Asset__c's own OWD and sharing rules independently of Account's sharing model, since Asset has an OWD setting distinct from Account and doesn't automatically inherit Account-level restrictions unless explicitly configured to do so (e.g., via a Master-Detail-like relationship or matching sharing rules)."), ("D", "Assets cannot have their own sharing rules under any circumstances; only Account-level rules can ever apply.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Asset has its own configurable OWD and sharing rules independent of Account, so achieving the technician-specific restriction requires deliberately configuring Asset's own sharing model rather than assuming automatic inheritance from Account. A is incorrect: Changing Account's OWD has no automatic effect on Asset's separately configured OWD. B is incorrect: Asset access doesn't automatically mirror Account; it has independently configurable sharing settings. D is incorrect: Asset fully supports its own sharing rules independent of Account."),
        Question(id: "116", question: "A global consultancy wants engagement teams to collaborate in Chatter on sensitive Engagement__c records, but worries that making a Chatter group private might inadvertently restrict record access for team members who need it, or that leaving it public might expose feed content beyond the team. What should the architect clarify about the relationship between Chatter group privacy and record-level access?", options: [("A", "Chatter group privacy settings and record sharing are the same underlying mechanism, so configuring one always configures the other identically."), ("B", "Making a Chatter group private automatically restricts record-level access on any record discussed within that group's feed to only group members."), ("C", "A public Chatter group automatically grants record-level access to any record mentioned or linked within its feed, regardless of the viewer's actual sharing access."), ("D", "Chatter group privacy and record-level sharing (OWD, sharing rules, etc.) are independent access layers — a private Chatter group restricts visibility of the group's feed content to its members, but has no effect on record-level access, and record-level sharing has no effect on Chatter group membership or feed visibility.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Chatter group privacy governs feed content visibility to group members, while record-level sharing independently governs record access — the two layers don't influence each other, so each must be configured deliberately for its own purpose. A is incorrect: These are distinct mechanisms serving different purposes, not a single shared setting. B is incorrect: Chatter group privacy has no bearing on the underlying record's sharing configuration. C is incorrect: A public Chatter group doesn't grant record-level access; that's governed separately by sharing."),
        Question(id: "117", question: "A university's admissions department created a custom list view on Application__c visible only to a specific public group, intending this as the sole access control for sensitive applicant data. A compliance review flags this as insufficient. What should the architect explain?", options: [("A", "List view visibility only controls who can find and use that particular list view; it does not restrict or expand the underlying record-level access, so relying on it as the sole control leaves the data accessible to anyone with independent object/record access through other list views, reports, or the API."), ("B", "List view visibility restrictions are equivalent to record-level sharing, so restricting the list view to one group is sufficient to fully secure the underlying data across every other access path."), ("C", "Once a list view is restricted to a group, the underlying Application__c records become automatically inaccessible to everyone else across the entire org."), ("D", "List views are the primary and only mechanism Salesforce provides for restricting access to sensitive custom object data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "List view visibility is purely about which users can use a specific list view; the underlying records remain governed by whatever actual object permissions and sharing exist, regardless of list view restriction. B is incorrect: List view restriction and record-level sharing are not equivalent; the former doesn't touch the latter. C is incorrect: Restricting one list view has zero effect on record accessibility through any other access path. D is incorrect: Object permissions, FLS, and sharing (OWD, rules, etc.) are the actual mechanisms for restricting sensitive data, not list views."),
        Question(id: "118", question: "A regulated energy utility must ensure that historical meter-reading data older than 10 years, now stored in a Big Object for scale, still supports a compliance audit requirement to trace exactly which internal team can currently query it. Given Big Objects' simplified permission model, how should the architect satisfy the audit requirement?", options: [("A", "Document and periodically review which specific profiles and permission sets have been granted Read (and Create, if applicable) on the Big Object, since that object-level permission grant is the entire access surface for Big Objects, with no additional sharing-rule layer to audit."), ("B", "Query the Big Object's associated Share table for RowCause entries, since Big Objects maintain a full Share object exactly like standard custom objects."), ("C", "Review the role hierarchy positions of every user in the org, since Big Object access is governed by hierarchy position rather than object permissions."), ("D", "Rely on Field History Tracking on the Big Object's fields, since that is the definitive source of who currently has query access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Since Big Objects are governed solely by object-level Read/Create permissions with no record-level sharing layer, a complete audit trail is achieved by reviewing exactly which profiles/permission sets hold that permission. B is incorrect: Big Objects don't have a Share object/table the way standard or custom objects do. C is incorrect: Big Objects aren't governed by role hierarchy position. D is incorrect: Field History Tracking records value changes, not who currently has query access."),
        Question(id: "119", question: "A wealth management firm's Trade_Order__c object has millions of records and a criteria-based sharing rule that shares orders flagged 'Compliance Review' with the Compliance team. During peak trading hours, bulk updates flip this flag on thousands of orders per minute, and the compliance team complains about sharing recalculation lag creating a poor experience. What architectural change should the architect consider first?", options: [("A", "Evaluate moving the compliance-flagging logic to an asynchronous Apex managed sharing process that inserts TradeOrderShare records directly and immediately for flagged orders, since a high-frequency criteria-based sharing rule recalculation at this volume can be a scalability bottleneck better addressed by more directly controlled programmatic sharing."), ("B", "Increase the criteria-based sharing rule's priority setting, since sharing rules support a priority field that eliminates recalculation lag entirely regardless of volume."), ("C", "Grant the Compliance team View All Data, since that is the only way to eliminate sharing recalculation lag under high-frequency updates."), ("D", "Disable sharing calculation for Trade_Order__c permanently, since recalculation is never necessary once initial sharing rules are configured.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "At very high update frequency, criteria-based sharing rule recalculation can become a bottleneck; moving to a directly controlled Apex managed sharing process can reduce lag by immediately inserting share records tied to the flagging event itself. B is incorrect: Sharing rules don't have a \"priority\" setting that eliminates recalculation lag. C is incorrect: View All Data is a broad, disproportionate grant relative to the actual performance problem. D is incorrect: Recalculation is necessary whenever sharing-relevant data changes; permanently disabling it would break the sharing model entirely."),
        Question(id: "120", question: "An architect is asked to summarize, for an executive audit, the native tools available to diagnose why a specific user currently has (or lacks) access to a specific record in a complex, multi-mechanism sharing model. Which TWO tools provide targeted, per-record/per-user diagnosis?", options: [("A", "The Record Access ('Why can I see this record?') / Sharing Hierarchy view."), ("B", "The Sharing Debugger."), ("C", "The Setup Audit Trail, since it comprehensively records every sharing source contributing to any user's access to any record at any point in time."), ("D", "Login History, since it logs a full breakdown of which sharing mechanisms applied during each user session.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "The Record Access / Sharing Hierarchy view (A) and the Sharing Debugger (B) are two distinct, independent native tools that each provide targeted, per-record, per-user access diagnosis. C is incorrect: The Setup Audit Trail records configuration changes, not per-record/per-user access attribution. D is incorrect: Login History has no visibility into per-record sharing sources.")
